WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.405 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.405 --> 00:00:04.329 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.329 --> 00:00:06.734 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:10.590 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.420 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So morphology.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.700 align:middle line:90%
Remember morphology?

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.700 align:middle line:84%
We were talking when we talked
about morphology about the fact

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.120 align:middle line:84%
that a word like
"unlockable" is ambiguous.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.290 align:middle line:84%
It can mean either it's
possible to unlock it,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.130 align:middle line:84%
or it is not
possible to lock it.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.285 align:middle line:84%
So it can be a desirable
property of a lock or not.

00:00:34.285 --> 00:00:35.910 align:middle line:84%
When we talked, I
think, about the fact

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.195 align:middle line:84%
that at least I
want to pronounce

00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:39.570 align:middle line:84%
it a little
differently depending

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.670 align:middle line:90%
on which of those things I say.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:48.670 align:middle line:84%
So The door is unlockable"
means it can be unlocked.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.180 align:middle line:84%
But the door is broken,
"It's unlockable,"

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.700 align:middle line:90%
means it cannot be locked.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.600 align:middle line:84%
I have this desire to put
an extra oomph on "un-"--

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.440 align:middle line:84%
an extra little
demi-stress beat on "un-"

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.815 align:middle line:84%
if I mean the
thing on the right.

00:01:01.815 --> 00:01:04.709 align:middle line:90%


00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.960 align:middle line:84%
And what we said, when we
were talking about this,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.345 align:middle line:84%
was we can account for
this kind of ambiguity

00:01:10.345 --> 00:01:11.220 align:middle line:90%
in the following way.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.130 align:middle line:84%
We'll say some things
which are clearly true.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:18.025 align:middle line:84%
First, "-able" combines with
verbs to make adjectives.

00:01:18.025 --> 00:01:19.650 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of
verbs that you can

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:20.940 align:middle line:90%
make into adjectives this way.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.815 align:middle line:84%
So you can take a verb
like "sing," or a verb

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.720 align:middle line:84%
like "understand"
and get adjectives

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.910 align:middle line:84%
like "singable" or
"understandable."

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.560 align:middle line:84%
And to say that
something is singable

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.830 align:middle line:84%
is to say that it's
possible to sing it, right?

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.100 align:middle line:90%
That's what that means.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.000 align:middle line:84%
"Understandable" means it's
possible to understand it.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.040 align:middle line:84%
So there's a "-able" suffix that
changes verbs into adjectives.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.850 align:middle line:84%
And then, we said,
there are two "un-"s.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:48.280 align:middle line:84%
There's an "un-" that combines
with verbs and makes verbs.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.370 align:middle line:84%
That's an "un-" that means
something like undo the effects

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:57.960 align:middle line:84%
of, or change something so that
it is no longer in the state

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.110 align:middle line:84%
that it would have been if
the verb had applied to it,

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.160 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.780 align:middle line:84%
So "untie" means "take something
and do things to it such

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.669 align:middle line:84%
that it is no
longer in the state

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:11.490 align:middle line:84%
that it is standardly in
after you have tied it."

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.920 align:middle line:84%
Or to put that a little more
briefly, "take something

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.860 align:middle line:84%
that was tied and make
it so it is not tied."

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.060 align:middle line:90%
That's what "untie" means.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.250 align:middle line:84%
Similarly for "undo" and
a bunch of other "un-"s.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:21.750 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.967 align:middle line:84%
So there's an "un-" that
applies to verbs, and has--

00:02:23.967 --> 00:02:25.800 align:middle line:84%
it's sometimes called
a reversitive meaning.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.060 align:middle line:84%
You change it so that
it's back from the state

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.810 align:middle line:90%
it would have been in.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.240 align:middle line:84%
And then there's another
"un-," let us call it "un-"

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.500 align:middle line:90%
number two.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.740 align:middle line:84%
"Un-" number two combines with
adjectives and makes adjectives

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:42.210 align:middle line:84%
that mean more or less
"not (adjective)."

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:46.200 align:middle line:84%
So "unkind," or
"unfamiliar," "unfortunate."

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.840 align:middle line:84%
These all mean not the
adjective, whatever it is.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.420 align:middle line:84%
And so what we
said was-- oh, hey.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.017 align:middle line:90%
Raquel?

00:02:52.017 --> 00:02:54.350 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I have a horrible
thought, and it's random.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.536 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, no.

00:02:55.536 --> 00:02:57.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But when you were
saying, put something so

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.550 align:middle line:84%
that it's no longer
tied, I would

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.580 align:middle line:84%
argue that not only
are we saying make it

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.130 align:middle line:84%
so it's no longer tied, but
leave it in a similar state

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.560 align:middle line:84%
that it was at the
beginning chronologically,

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.110 align:middle line:84%
because you can chop a shoelace
in lots of little pieces,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.600 align:middle line:90%
and it's no longer tied.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.363 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:14.363 --> 00:03:16.280 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh,
well that's a nice point.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:16.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.390 align:middle line:90%
So if I take--

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.020 align:middle line:90%
let's see.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.920 align:middle line:84%
If I untie a shoelace, first of
all, it has to start off tied,

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:25.880 align:middle line:90%
is that right?

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.350 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a shoelace which
is not tied, I can't untie it.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.410 align:middle line:84%
But if it's tied and
then I take scissors

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:40.130 align:middle line:84%
and I cut it into many small
pieces, have I untied it?

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.600 align:middle line:90%
No, surely not.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.660 align:middle line:90%


00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.610 align:middle line:84%
When Alexander cut
the Gordian knot,

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.620 align:middle line:90%
he wasn't untying the knot.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.400 align:middle line:84%
He was being more
direct than that.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.763 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:03:54.763 --> 00:03:56.680 align:middle line:84%
So you have to put it--
so what you just said,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.030 align:middle line:90%
I like the way you just said it.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:03:59.530 align:middle line:84%
You have to put it
back in the state

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:02.467 align:middle line:84%
that it was in
before it was tied.

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:03.550 align:middle line:90%
Is that the way to say it?

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.230 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Going back and
tie it, [? essentially. ?]

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:06.147 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:04:06.147 --> 00:04:06.880 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.870 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, that's a nice point.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.018 align:middle line:90%
Undo.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:14.560 align:middle line:84%
I mean, because what we said--
we talked about this in class,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.640 align:middle line:90%
that there are lots of things.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.019 align:middle line:90%
That it's hard to do this.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:22.600 align:middle line:84%
This "un-" is kind of picky
about what it can combine with.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.270 align:middle line:84%
So the "un-" that attaches to
adjectives can attach to lots

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.880 align:middle line:90%
of adjectives, but you can't--

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.680 align:middle line:84%
if I take some shoes, I can't
unwash shoes, or unwash socks.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.810 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't mean
"take the socks

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.850 align:middle line:90%
and make them dirty again."

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.675 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine that it would,
but that's not what it means.

00:04:41.675 --> 00:04:43.550 align:middle line:84%
And maybe that's related
to your observation.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.310 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's interesting
to think about.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.100 align:middle line:90%
Joseph, did you have a--

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.236 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I was going to--

00:04:50.236 --> 00:04:55.500 align:middle line:84%
based on what Raquel said, does
the final-- after you "un-"

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.860 align:middle line:84%
something, is that going
to be able to be redone?

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.415 align:middle line:84%
So if I untie a shoelace
by cutting it up, now--

00:05:02.415 --> 00:05:03.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.870 align:middle line:90%
It can't be tied again.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.390 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's see.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:13.650 align:middle line:84%
If you undo an
operation on a computer,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.900 align:middle line:84%
does it have to be possible
to do the operation again?

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.980 align:middle line:90%
I don't know, maybe.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:19.570 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there is a
redo, isn't there?

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:20.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.123 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I wonder.

00:05:22.123 --> 00:05:23.290 align:middle line:90%
Maybe that is what it means.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:27.160 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it means-- yeah.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.680 align:middle line:84%
And as you say, this is
related to Raquel's point,

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.180 align:middle line:84%
you have to put
it in a state such

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.662 align:middle line:84%
that the verb could
apply to it again, maybe.

00:05:33.662 --> 00:05:35.080 align:middle line:90%
Maybe.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.610 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, so there's a lot.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.910 align:middle line:84%
What I actually did on the
slide was just to say, "un-"

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.800 align:middle line:84%
number one combines with
verbs to make verbs.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.870 align:middle line:84%
And then my mental
notes to myself

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.330 align:middle line:84%
say, vamp something
about what it means.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.310 align:middle line:84%
You guys are doing some
sophisticated thinking

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:49.480 align:middle line:90%
about what it means.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:51.920 align:middle line:84%
It's a little complicated,
figuring out what it means,

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:52.780 align:middle line:90%
as you can see.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.030 align:middle line:90%
Nice point.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.330 align:middle line:84%
Still, what I've said on the
slide, apart from the vamping,

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.120 align:middle line:90%
I think is true.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.370 align:middle line:84%
There's an "-able" that
changes verbs into adjectives,

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.520 align:middle line:84%
and there are "un-"s, one
that combines with verbs,

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.270 align:middle line:84%
and another that
combines with adjectives.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:07.390 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right so far?

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:12.400 align:middle line:84%
And what all of this means is
the ambiguity of "unlockable,"

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.190 align:middle line:84%
we get to attribute it to
basically the fact that there

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.860 align:middle line:90%
are two "un-"s.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:19.990 align:middle line:84%
So you could have
attached the "un-"

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.480 align:middle line:84%
before you attached the "-able,"
or you could have attached

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:24.250 align:middle line:84%
the "un-" after you
attached the "-able."

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.470 align:middle line:84%
Because the "-able" is
going to change a verb

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:29.380 align:middle line:84%
into an adjective, and "un-"
can combine with either a verb

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.250 align:middle line:90%
or an adjective.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:31.750 align:middle line:84%
So that was the way
we were talking.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:38.650 align:middle line:84%
So the idea was you can start by
attaching "un-" on number one,

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:44.052 align:middle line:84%
so "lock," giving you
this new verb, "unlock,"

00:06:44.052 --> 00:06:45.760 align:middle line:84%
which is related to
the meaning of "lock"

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.040 align:middle line:84%
in mysterious and complicated
ways that we've now been

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.570 align:middle line:90%
talking about.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.840 align:middle line:84%
And then you can attach
"-able" to that and give you

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.970 align:middle line:90%
an adjective, "unlockable."

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.610 align:middle line:84%
And I drew trees
like this before

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:59.110 align:middle line:84%
and said, yeah,
these trees are kind

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.660 align:middle line:84%
of a representation of the
order in which you did things.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:03.910 align:middle line:84%
That's all they're
for, is to say

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:07.150 align:middle line:84%
you started by putting
together those two

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.670 align:middle line:84%
things at the bottom of the
tree, the verb and that prefix,

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:11.590 align:middle line:90%
and you created a verb.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:15.790 align:middle line:84%
That's what that prefix does, it
takes verbs and returns verbs.

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:19.180 align:middle line:84%
And then that verb gets to
attach the second thing you do.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:20.620 align:middle line:84%
That verb attaches
to the suffix,

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:24.910 align:middle line:84%
and now the suffix changes
that verb into an adjective.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.030 align:middle line:84%
Or you can do things
in the other order.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.130 align:middle line:84%
You can attach
"-able" to "lock,"

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.740 align:middle line:84%
giving you an adjective,
and you can take "un-"

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:36.550 align:middle line:84%
number two and attach "un-"
number two to that adjective,

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.730 align:middle line:84%
giving you a new adjective
meaning "not lockable."

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:41.980 align:middle line:84%
And so "unlockable"
is ambiguous.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:45.677 align:middle line:84%
And the ambiguity, we said,
comes from the fact that, well,

00:07:45.677 --> 00:07:48.010 align:middle line:84%
there are two "un-"s, which
is something we can observe.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:49.480 align:middle line:84%
There's an "un-" that
goes on verbs and an "un-"

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.430 align:middle line:84%
that goes on adjectives,
and there's a "-able"

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.800 align:middle line:84%
that changes verbs
to adjectives, right?

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:54.640 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.430 align:middle line:84%
And that means that
there's an "un-"

00:07:57.430 --> 00:07:59.597 align:middle line:84%
that can go before the
"-able," and there's an "un-"

00:07:59.597 --> 00:08:02.097 align:middle line:84%
that can go after the "-able,"
and so we get this ambiguity.

00:08:02.097 --> 00:08:04.270 align:middle line:84%
And the ambiguity is what
we would expect it to be.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:05.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.477 align:middle line:90%
This is all review.

00:08:07.477 --> 00:08:09.810 align:middle line:84%
Having said all this, I guess
what we expect is that you

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.810 align:middle line:84%
could have two "un-"s, I didn't
put this on the slide anywhere,

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:16.410 align:middle line:84%
that it would be possible to
say it is "un-unlockable,"

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.110 align:middle line:90%
which I think is true, actually.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:22.030 align:middle line:90%
"This door is broken.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.160 align:middle line:90%
It is un-unlockable."

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:26.520 align:middle line:84%
That would mean it
cannot be unlocked.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.470 align:middle line:90%
I think that's true.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.020 align:middle line:84%
Go ask some people whose
minds have not been

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:32.340 align:middle line:90%
contaminated by linguistics.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:36.570 align:middle line:84%
Go harass your
roommates or whoever.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:39.370 align:middle line:84%
You'll make yourself
popular that way.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.370 align:middle line:84%
And if you are going to
try to learn from me how

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:42.900 align:middle line:84%
to make yourself
popular, then boy,

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.870 align:middle line:90%
are you in the wrong class.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.900 align:middle line:90%
OK, is this all clear?

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.970 align:middle line:84%
So the important
part of the story

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.030 align:middle line:84%
is to say, yeah,
"unlockable" is a word.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.430 align:middle line:84%
It's got three morphemes in
it, a prefix, and a root,

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:57.720 align:middle line:90%
and a suffix.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:00.030 align:middle line:84%
But it isn't just three
morphemes in a row.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.850 align:middle line:84%
Those morphemes were
assembled in an order.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:04.920 align:middle line:90%
You assembled them pairwise.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.510 align:middle line:84%
You first put two
of them together,

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:09.270 align:middle line:84%
and then you added
another one to the result

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:13.200 align:middle line:84%
of that first putting together,
and that order has consequences

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.510 align:middle line:90%
for interpretation.

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.270 align:middle line:84%
So it's not false to
say that unlockable

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:21.960 align:middle line:84%
consists of three morphemes, a
prefix, a stem, and a suffix.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:23.880 align:middle line:84%
But it's not a
complete description

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.500 align:middle line:90%
of what's happened either.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.460 align:middle line:84%
It is that, but it
is also those trees,

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.350 align:middle line:84%
or those trees represent
something, namely

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.160 align:middle line:84%
the order in which
you did things.

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:35.810 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.430 align:middle line:84%
Now what we're
going to do now is

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.930 align:middle line:84%
start talking
about syntax, which

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:40.555 align:middle line:84%
is the study of how
words are assembled

00:09:40.555 --> 00:09:44.510 align:middle line:84%
to make sentences, words,
sometimes things smaller

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:45.720 align:middle line:90%
than words, as we'll see.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.720 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to see
that it's useful to think

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.300 align:middle line:84%
of sentences as
being put together

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:55.160 align:middle line:84%
in a bunch of operations more or
less the way "unlockable" is--

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.810 align:middle line:84%
that we take pairs of
words and put them together

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:05.840 align:middle line:84%
to form larger objects the way
I just did for "unlockable."

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.960 align:middle line:90%


00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.150 align:middle line:84%
Just to give you an example
of the kind of thing

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:12.567 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
talk about, I think

00:10:12.567 --> 00:10:16.040 align:middle line:84%
I talked with you about
this on the first day.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.700 align:middle line:84%
Just as with
"unlockable," it is true,

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:21.410 align:middle line:84%
but it is not a
complete description

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.870 align:middle line:84%
to say that is three morphemes,
a prefix, and a stem,

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:25.130 align:middle line:90%
and a suffix.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:27.380 align:middle line:84%
That's true, but it's not
a complete description.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.660 align:middle line:84%
A complete description
involves those trees

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:33.530 align:middle line:84%
or some equivalent
explanation of the order

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.090 align:middle line:90%
in which you did things.

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:38.810 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, these two sentences,
"John walked up the stairs,"

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.270 align:middle line:84%
and "Mary looked up the
reference," it's true,

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:42.980 align:middle line:84%
but it is not a
complete description

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:44.660 align:middle line:84%
to say that those are
two sentences that

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:48.230 align:middle line:84%
consist of a noun, a verb,
a preposition, a determiner,

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:50.680 align:middle line:90%
and a noun.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.530 align:middle line:84%
Did I say anything that
alarmed anybody just now?

00:10:53.530 --> 00:10:58.330 align:middle line:84%
So those are two sentences
that consist of five words.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:10:59.290 align:middle line:90%
That's true.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.945 align:middle line:84%
And we can say things about
what kinds of words they are.

00:11:01.945 --> 00:11:04.360 align:middle line:84%
There are nouns up
there, and verbs,

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:08.410 align:middle line:84%
and prepositions like "up," and
I just called it a determiner,

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:09.010 align:middle line:90%
"the."

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:10.990 align:middle line:84%
People sometimes call
it the definite article.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:15.430 align:middle line:84%
You'll hear me call it the
determiner a lot in this class.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:18.580 align:middle line:84%
It's true to say that those two
sentences consist of those five

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:19.090 align:middle line:90%
words.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:21.485 align:middle line:84%
But just as with the two
versions of "unlockable,"

00:11:21.485 --> 00:11:23.110 align:middle line:84%
we can convince
ourselves that it's not

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.610 align:middle line:84%
a complete
description, that it's

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:28.210 align:middle line:84%
helpful to think about the
order in which you assembled

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.290 align:middle line:90%
these things.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:33.790 align:middle line:84%
So what I told you last
time was, effectively

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:35.980 align:middle line:84%
what we're going
to want to say is,

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:38.410 align:middle line:84%
there was an
operation that created

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:43.000 align:middle line:84%
the substring "up the stairs"
in that first sentence.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.430 align:middle line:84%
That's what we
call a constituent.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:49.810 align:middle line:84%
And there is no similar
operation creating a substring

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:52.480 align:middle line:84%
"up the reference"
in the second one.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:57.070 align:middle line:84%
And again, this is review, but
it's review from the first day.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:00.280 align:middle line:84%
What I convinced you, I
think, I hope, I tried,

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:03.820 align:middle line:84%
was that there are various
syntactic phenomena,

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:08.650 align:middle line:84%
various things you get to
do with sentences that treat

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:11.620 align:middle line:84%
"up the stairs"
as a single object

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:14.253 align:middle line:84%
that syntax gets to
manipulate in various ways.

00:12:14.253 --> 00:12:15.670 align:middle line:84%
We're going be
talking about that,

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:17.920 align:middle line:90%
about what that means exactly.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.700 align:middle line:84%
And the same
syntactic operations

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:24.680 align:middle line:84%
don't get to treat "up the
reference" as a single thing.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:29.080 align:middle line:84%
So we said it's possible
to ask questions like, "Up

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.150 align:middle line:90%
which stairs did John walk?"

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:33.160 align:middle line:90%
It's fairly stuffy questions.

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:36.340 align:middle line:84%
It's a strange way to ask the
question, but you can say it.

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:39.430 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to, "Up which
reference did Mary look?"

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:41.890 align:middle line:90%
which is gibberish.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:44.080 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
draw a distinction.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:46.390 align:middle line:84%
This is maybe the
first time that I've

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:48.910 align:middle line:84%
shown you a case where
syntacticians have

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.430 align:middle line:84%
to care passionately about
the difference between one

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:53.140 align:middle line:84%
sentence, which is
complete gibberish,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:55.210 align:middle line:84%
and another sentence,
which is not great.

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.220 align:middle line:84%
There's a fair amount
of great syntax

00:12:57.220 --> 00:12:59.920 align:middle line:84%
that's built on those
kinds of distinctions.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.830 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, if I say,
"John walked up the stairs,"

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:05.440 align:middle line:84%
and you're surprised for
some reason, you can say,

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:06.370 align:middle line:90%
"Up the stairs?"

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.350 align:middle line:84%
That's not a weird
thing for you to say.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:10.810 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if I say, "Mary
looked up the reference,"

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.720 align:middle line:84%
and you're surprised, no
matter how surprised you are,

00:13:13.720 --> 00:13:16.120 align:middle line:84%
you're not going to
say, "Up the reference?"

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:19.210 align:middle line:90%
That's a weird response.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:22.900 align:middle line:84%
So the point is, just
as with "unlockable,"

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:27.670 align:middle line:84%
yeah, it's three morphemes,
prefix, stem, suffix.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:30.250 align:middle line:84%
But having said that, we
haven't said everything.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:34.180 align:middle line:84%
We have to know which parts of
"unlockable" are single parts.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:37.030 align:middle line:84%
Is it unattached to
"lockable," or is it "unlock"

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:38.570 align:middle line:90%
with a "-able" attached to it?

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:42.190 align:middle line:84%
Those are different adjectives
with different meanings.

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:44.770 align:middle line:84%
Similarly here, it's
not enough to say, yeah,

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:47.450 align:middle line:84%
we've got these five
words of these types.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:49.570 align:middle line:84%
We've got to know
which of these things

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:53.200 align:middle line:84%
go together, which things
are parts, single parts.

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:55.570 align:middle line:84%
And there is a part,
"up the stairs,"

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:58.000 align:middle line:84%
what we call a constituent,
that various kinds

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.650 align:middle line:84%
of syntactic
phenomena care about,

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:03.790 align:middle line:84%
like the syntactic
phenomenon can I

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:05.410 align:middle line:90%
repeat this if I'm astonished?

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:06.790 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's a test--

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.760 align:middle line:84%
kind of test for this
property of constituenthood.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So there's more to a
sentence than its parts.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:13.450 align:middle line:84%
We've got to know,
in what order did

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:17.750 align:middle line:84%
you put those parts together,
just like with "unlockable."

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:19.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:21.490 align:middle line:90%
Makes sense?

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:24.760 align:middle line:90%
So we're going to do syntax.

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:28.440 align:middle line:84%
We want a theory that's
going to divide sentences

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.200 align:middle line:90%
into three kinds.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.822 align:middle line:84%
There are, on the
one hand, sentences

00:14:32.822 --> 00:14:34.530 align:middle line:84%
that you've heard a
zillion times before,

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:36.420 align:middle line:90%
like "We're going to class."

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:40.890 align:middle line:84%
And on the other hand,
sentences that you have possibly

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:44.160 align:middle line:84%
never heard anyone
say, but that are fine.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:47.250 align:middle line:84%
So if I say, "My anteater
is hula dancing,"

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:50.910 align:middle line:84%
you may never in your life
have heard anyone say that.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:51.660 align:middle line:90%
Maybe you have.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:55.110 align:middle line:84%
Some of you may have had more
exciting lives than I have.

00:14:55.110 --> 00:14:58.710 align:middle line:90%
But it's an OK sentence.

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:01.470 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to, "We're
class going to,"

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:02.810 align:middle line:90%
which I've given a star there.

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:04.920 align:middle line:84%
Recall that the star
is what syntacticians

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:06.700 align:middle line:90%
give to things that are bad.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:09.630 align:middle line:84%
So syntacticians are the
opposite of normal people.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:12.930 align:middle line:84%
When we see things we don't
like, we give them a gold star.

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:13.930 align:middle line:90%
Usually, it's not gold.

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:14.460 align:middle line:90%
It's black.

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.085 align:middle line:84%
I guess that makes
a little more sense.

00:15:16.085 --> 00:15:18.520 align:middle line:90%


00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.857 align:middle line:84%
This three-way distinction
is worth highlighting,

00:15:21.857 --> 00:15:23.940 align:middle line:84%
because if I had only given
you the first sentence

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.770 align:middle line:84%
and the second sentence,
"We're going to class,"

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:28.170 align:middle line:84%
and "We're class going to,"
the first sentence is good,

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.500 align:middle line:84%
and the second-- and the last
sentence is bad, some of you

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.260 align:middle line:84%
might be thinking, well,
that first sentence,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:36.930 align:middle line:84%
it's a sentence I've heard
lots of times before.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.600 align:middle line:84%
Maybe when I was a small child,
I heard my parents say that.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:43.460 align:middle line:84%
And I heard them say it
and I remember it now.

00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:44.490 align:middle line:90%
And maybe that's all.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.190 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's what syntax is,
it's the ability to remember

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.800 align:middle line:90%
things you've heard people say.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:53.370 align:middle line:84%
But the existence of the
second class of sentences

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:56.100 align:middle line:90%
shows that that's hopeless.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:59.610 align:middle line:84%
So you're not just remembering
things you've heard people say

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:02.970 align:middle line:84%
when you're deciding which
things deserve the black star

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:04.950 align:middle line:90%
and which things don't.

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:07.413 align:middle line:84%
You're not just categorizing
sentences into sentences

00:16:07.413 --> 00:16:09.330 align:middle line:84%
you've heard before and
sentences you haven't.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:13.140 align:middle line:84%
You've got this intuition about
which sentences are acceptable.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:15.150 align:middle line:84%
And it's not just
about which sentences

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:17.010 align:middle line:84%
are in your input,
which sentences

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:18.330 align:middle line:90%
you've heard people say.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:20.220 align:middle line:90%
It's something else.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.230 align:middle line:84%
We're going to try to
figure out what that is.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.450 align:middle line:84%
But I'm giving you
these three sentences

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.960 align:middle line:84%
to slay a hypothesis that
you might be entertaining.

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.150 align:middle line:84%
Stop entertaining
that hypothesis.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:31.320 align:middle line:90%
Make it go home.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.420 align:middle line:90%
It's not a good hypothesis.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:34.620 align:middle line:90%
It won't do you any good.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:35.370 align:middle line:90%
Is that clear?

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:36.787 align:middle line:84%
Are people clear
on the hypothesis

00:16:36.787 --> 00:16:38.580 align:middle line:90%
that I'm attempting to slay?

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:39.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:43.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe to [INAUDIBLE]
a modified version, what if it's

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:45.390 align:middle line:84%
not particular sentences
that we're remembering,

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:49.833 align:middle line:84%
but structures of sentences
that we've built up over time?

00:16:49.833 --> 00:16:50.750 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:53.905 align:middle line:84%
So if we pursued that--
that's that's much more

00:16:53.905 --> 00:16:55.280 align:middle line:84%
sophisticated than
the hypothesis

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:56.450 align:middle line:90%
I was trying to slay.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:59.783 align:middle line:84%
So this is day one
of syntax So no,

00:16:59.783 --> 00:17:00.950 align:middle line:90%
you're raising a good point.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:04.490 align:middle line:84%
What if all you're
doing is remembering

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:05.359 align:middle line:90%
chunks of sentences?

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:07.700 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you've never heard
anybody say, "My anteater

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.170 align:middle line:84%
is hula dancing,"
but maybe you've

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:11.150 align:middle line:90%
heard people say "anteater."

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.839 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you've heard people
say "My anteater," maybe not.

00:17:14.839 --> 00:17:17.510 align:middle line:84%
But at least you've heard
people say "My (noun),"

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:20.000 align:middle line:84%
and you've heard
people say "anteater,"

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.010 align:middle line:90%
and "is hula dancing."

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:22.513 align:middle line:90%
Well, yeah.

00:17:22.513 --> 00:17:24.680 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you've heard somebody
say something like that.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:27.230 align:middle line:84%
So maybe there are some
parts of this that you

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:28.670 align:middle line:90%
could be acquiring that way.

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:30.605 align:middle line:90%
We're going to have to be--

00:17:30.605 --> 00:17:32.980 align:middle line:84%
we're going to have to pursue
that hypothesis long enough

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:36.820 align:middle line:84%
to find out exactly what it
says, right because what I just

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.030 align:middle line:84%
said, which wasn't
really the hypothesis,

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:42.315 align:middle line:84%
it was an attempt
to represent it,

00:17:42.315 --> 00:17:43.690 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
have to figure out

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:47.320 align:middle line:84%
how to rule out "We're class
going to," because you probably

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:51.430 align:middle line:84%
have heard people say
"we're," and "class,"

00:17:51.430 --> 00:17:53.680 align:middle line:84%
and you've heard
people say "going to."

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:56.170 align:middle line:84%
Someone asks, "Where
are you going to?"

00:17:56.170 --> 00:17:57.590 align:middle line:90%
We've heard people say that.

00:17:57.590 --> 00:18:00.820 align:middle line:84%
And so we're going to have
to be careful about that

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:02.710 align:middle line:84%
hypothesis to try
to figure out how

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:04.450 align:middle line:90%
we could use it to rule and--

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:07.240 align:middle line:84%
to draw the distinctions
that are on the board.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:08.260 align:middle line:90%
But you're right.

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:12.730 align:middle line:84%
So just to summarize this
conversation you and I have

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:16.400 align:middle line:84%
just had, I said, there's
a fairly stupid hypothesis,

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:17.650 align:middle line:90%
which says--

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:20.020 align:middle line:84%
and I'm raising it
partly because it's

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:23.050 align:middle line:84%
been seriously entertained in
the literature before, which

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:26.350 align:middle line:84%
says, all you're doing
is remembering things

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.012 align:middle line:84%
people have said,
and that's what

00:18:28.012 --> 00:18:29.470 align:middle line:84%
distinguishes
grammatical sentences

00:18:29.470 --> 00:18:31.030 align:middle line:90%
from interpretable sentences.

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:32.450 align:middle line:90%
That's false.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:35.080 align:middle line:84%
So we have this distinction
between sentences

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.360 align:middle line:84%
that are grammatical
and sentences that

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:39.112 align:middle line:84%
are ungrammatical
that isn't just

00:18:39.112 --> 00:18:41.320 align:middle line:84%
a list of all the sentences
you've ever heard before.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.362 align:middle line:84%
You can take a sentence
you've never heard before

00:18:43.362 --> 00:18:45.940 align:middle line:90%
and accept it.

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:47.440 align:middle line:84%
You're raising the
point there could

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.930 align:middle line:84%
be a better version of the
stupid hypothesis, one that

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:51.790 align:middle line:84%
said, well, maybe
there are some parts

00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:54.610 align:middle line:84%
of the sentence that you've
heard before, which is true.

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:56.860 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have to
be explicit about which

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:59.918 align:middle line:84%
subparts count and what exactly
we mean when we say that.

00:18:59.918 --> 00:19:01.960 align:middle line:84%
But you're right, there
could be a better version

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:03.830 align:middle line:90%
of that hypothesis.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:05.020 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:06.653 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:19:06.653 --> 00:19:08.320 align:middle line:84%
Did I successfully
answer your question?

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:11.490 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:14.760 align:middle line:90%


00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:20.388 align:middle line:84%
So let's talk about what's wrong
with "We're class going to."

00:19:20.388 --> 00:19:24.820 align:middle line:84%
They have several hypotheses
about what's wrong with it.

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:27.890 align:middle line:84%
One could be that it
doesn't mean anything.

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:30.040 align:middle line:84%
It's a thing people
sometimes say.

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:32.140 align:middle line:84%
When I'm trying to
explain to people--

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:34.810 align:middle line:84%
when I'm on an airplane
flights and people ask me,

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:37.790 align:middle line:90%
what do you do for a living?

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:39.490 align:middle line:84%
How I answer
depends on whether I

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:42.033 align:middle line:84%
feel like talking to
the person or not.

00:19:42.033 --> 00:19:43.450 align:middle line:84%
So if I feel like
talking to them,

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:46.070 align:middle line:84%
then I will tell them that I
work on endangered languages,

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:47.740 align:middle line:84%
which is something
I do I work with,

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:49.930 align:middle line:84%
languages that are down to
their last few speakers,

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:51.160 align:middle line:90%
and try to work with them.

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.110 align:middle line:84%
And then sometimes
they're interested in that

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:53.943 align:middle line:90%
and they talk to me.

00:19:53.943 --> 00:19:56.410 align:middle line:84%
If I would like to
get them to leave me

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:58.278 align:middle line:84%
alone so that I can
read a book or whatever,

00:19:58.278 --> 00:20:00.070 align:middle line:84%
I tell them I'm a
theoretical syntactician.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:04.310 align:middle line:84%
That usually ends the
conversation fairly quickly.

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.245 align:middle line:84%
But when it doesn't, when they
say, "Oh, what's that mean?

00:20:07.245 --> 00:20:08.120 align:middle line:90%
What do you work on?"

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:11.147 align:middle line:84%
Then I will say, well, I'm
trying to figure out why some

00:20:11.147 --> 00:20:12.980 align:middle line:84%
sentences are grammatical
and others aren't.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:13.938 align:middle line:90%
And I'll give examples.

00:20:13.938 --> 00:20:16.450 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes they will say,
"Oh, but what does that mean?"

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:17.913 align:middle line:90%
"We're class going to."

00:20:17.913 --> 00:20:19.330 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's what's
wrong with it.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:21.430 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't mean anything.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:27.220 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is we're
capable of distinguishing

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:30.310 align:middle line:84%
grammaticality,
even in sentences

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:33.170 align:middle line:90%
that don't mean anything.

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:37.285 align:middle line:84%
So this is a famous
example of Noam Chomsky's.

00:20:37.285 --> 00:20:39.910 align:middle line:84%
In fact, I think if you look him
up in like Bartlett's Familiar

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:42.760 align:middle line:84%
Quotations, you'll find
this first sentence,

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:45.585 align:middle line:84%
"Colorless green ideas
sleep furiously."

00:20:45.585 --> 00:20:46.960 align:middle line:84%
He actually offered
that sentence

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:48.670 align:middle line:84%
as a part of a
pair of sentences.

00:20:48.670 --> 00:20:51.910 align:middle line:84%
He wanted people to
contrast that sentence

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:54.610 align:middle line:84%
with the same sentence
backwards: "Furiously

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:56.680 align:middle line:90%
sleep ideas green colorless."

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:58.840 align:middle line:90%
So consider those two sentences.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:03.130 align:middle line:84%
And the point is neither
of them means anything.

00:21:03.130 --> 00:21:08.420 align:middle line:84%
So the first one
doesn't mean anything.

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:11.200 align:middle line:84%
And then if you turn your
attention to the second one,

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:13.060 align:middle line:90%
it also doesn't mean anything.

00:21:13.060 --> 00:21:15.350 align:middle line:84%
But they have a
different status.

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:17.920 align:middle line:84%
So the second one
doesn't mean anything

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:21.400 align:middle line:84%
and it's ungrammatical,
whereas the first one,

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:24.220 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't mean anything,
but you feel as though--

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:26.830 align:middle line:84%
and this is what I'm slowing
down right about here

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:28.720 align:middle line:84%
where I say it
doesn't mean anything.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.310 align:middle line:84%
Because the reaction
people sometimes

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:31.520 align:middle line:90%
have right about here--

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:33.680 align:middle line:84%
does anyone want-- here,
sometimes people say,

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:34.900 align:middle line:90%
"Oh, but look.

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:40.540 align:middle line:84%
Suppose 'colorless' meant
'boring,' and suppose

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:44.530 align:middle line:84%
'green' meant
'environmentalist,' and suppose

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.570 align:middle line:84%
'sleep' and 'furiously'
meant different--

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:49.000 align:middle line:84%
suppose these words meant
something other than what they

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:51.250 align:middle line:90%
mean.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:55.318 align:middle line:84%
Then the sentence would mean
something!"-- which is true.

00:21:55.318 --> 00:21:58.900 align:middle line:84%
But it's another way of
saying the same point.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:02.710 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, the first sentence,
"Colorless green ideas

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:05.650 align:middle line:84%
sleep furiously," is
meaningless if you don't mess

00:22:05.650 --> 00:22:07.630 align:middle line:84%
with the meanings
of the sentences.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:11.600 align:middle line:84%
But it obeys the rules for
how words can be combined.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:13.180 align:middle line:84%
If the words meant
something else,

00:22:13.180 --> 00:22:15.800 align:middle line:90%
the sentence would be fine.

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:17.320 align:middle line:84%
We can have English
sentences that

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:21.380 align:middle line:84%
consist of two adjectives
modifying a noun.

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:24.130 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a verb,
and then there's an adverb.

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:32.560 align:middle line:84%
Not that one, but "Big green
monsters snore loudly,"

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:33.370 align:middle line:90%
that would be fine.

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:36.110 align:middle line:90%


00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:40.480 align:middle line:84%
So when the person on the
airplane flight next to me

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:44.500 align:middle line:84%
says, "Oh, what's wrong
with 'We're class going to'

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:47.133 align:middle line:84%
is that it's a meaningless
sentence," if I'm

00:22:47.133 --> 00:22:49.550 align:middle line:84%
really, really desperately
trying to end the conversation,

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:51.370 align:middle line:84%
I bring out these
kinds of pairs.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:56.140 align:middle line:90%
So it's not about meaning.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:23:00.130 align:middle line:84%
We have this intuition that
there are sentences that are OK

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:03.070 align:middle line:84%
and sentences that are bad,
which is separable from

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:06.657 align:middle line:84%
our intuition about what means
something and what doesn't.

00:23:06.657 --> 00:23:08.740 align:middle line:84%
I've just been asserting
things about our feelings

00:23:08.740 --> 00:23:09.460 align:middle line:90%
about these sentences.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:11.500 align:middle line:84%
Do people have this feeling
about these sentences?

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:13.333 align:middle line:84%
First, that they're
meaningless, and second,

00:23:13.333 --> 00:23:17.530 align:middle line:84%
that the first one is OK
and the second one is bad?

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:18.395 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Raquel?

00:23:18.395 --> 00:23:20.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I can't remember
the word for this,

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:22.240 align:middle line:84%
but you were saying that
certain types of words

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:24.640 align:middle line:84%
are categories that you
can add more words to,

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:27.525 align:middle line:84%
even if you don't know
what they like modifiers

00:23:27.525 --> 00:23:28.400 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:31.987 align:middle line:84%
And like, "This house is very
[blope?]" That's grammatical,

00:23:31.987 --> 00:23:34.570 align:middle line:84%
even if you don't know what it
means, or you can't make that--

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:35.612 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, yes.

00:23:35.612 --> 00:23:36.640 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:41.980 align:middle line:84%
We were talking about open class
and closed class morphemes.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:43.630 align:middle line:84%
So Jabberwocky is
a poem that you

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:47.350 align:middle line:84%
can write, changing
all the lexical items

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:49.940 align:middle line:84%
to nonsense words,
but you couldn't

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:51.190 align:middle line:90%
do that with functional items.

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:53.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that's right.

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:57.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So maybe in situations
where something doesn't really

00:23:57.100 --> 00:24:00.550 align:middle line:84%
make any sense, your
brain still knows that OK,

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:02.980 align:middle line:84%
maybe it almost like
that kind of class

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:04.480 align:middle line:84%
where you're like,
well, I mean, you

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:06.340 align:middle line:84%
can fit like an
adjective like this--

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:06.660 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:08.702 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --even if it
means something ridiculous

00:24:08.702 --> 00:24:12.273 align:middle line:84%
right here, and it sounds
OK even if it's [INAUDIBLE]..

00:24:12.273 --> 00:24:13.190 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:14.545 align:middle line:90%
That's a nice way to put it.

00:24:14.545 --> 00:24:15.920 align:middle line:84%
I guess this is
similar to what I

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:18.650 align:middle line:84%
was trying to say about
a reaction I sometimes

00:24:18.650 --> 00:24:20.995 align:middle line:84%
get to this sentence,
which is, you

00:24:20.995 --> 00:24:22.940 align:middle line:84%
say "Colorless green
ideas sleep furiously"

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:24.360 align:middle line:90%
doesn't mean anything.

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:25.920 align:middle line:84%
And people will
sometimes say, "Well,

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:27.650 align:middle line:84%
but if these words
meant something else,

00:24:27.650 --> 00:24:30.590 align:middle line:84%
then it would be
OK," which is true.

00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:31.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:32.505 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I wonder
if it just has

00:24:32.505 --> 00:24:44.413 align:middle line:90%
to do with the idea [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:44.413 --> 00:24:45.330 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:47.560 align:middle line:84%
I think I may not have
heard all of that,

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.070 align:middle line:84%
but the idea was
the first sentence,

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:52.980 align:middle line:84%
it's clear what "colorless"
and "green" are trying to do.

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:54.990 align:middle line:84%
They're both trying
to modify ideas.

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:56.640 align:middle line:90%
Is that right?

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.930 align:middle line:84%
And it's clear what
"furiously" is trying to do.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:25:01.290 align:middle line:84%
It's an adverb and it's
trying to modify "sleep."

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:02.700 align:middle line:84%
When I say it's
meaningless, what

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:05.550 align:middle line:84%
I mean is you can't
sleep furiously, right?

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:08.130 align:middle line:84%
And things can't be both
colorless and green.

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:11.520 align:middle line:84%
And if they could, well, ideas
couldn't have those things.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:12.630 align:middle line:90%
They're abstract, right?

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:17.520 align:middle line:84%
That's the sense in which this
is a meaningless sentence.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:21.390 align:middle line:84%
I think you're raising the
point, which is a good point,

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:24.600 align:middle line:84%
yeah, it's meaningless,
but the words in it

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:26.730 align:middle line:84%
are fitting together
the way they should.

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:29.130 align:middle line:84%
The adjectives are going
where adjectives go.

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:32.160 align:middle line:84%
They go before the noun, that's
what they're supposed to do.

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:36.270 align:middle line:84%
And in the second sentence,
they're not doing that.

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:38.890 align:middle line:84%
Is that the relation to the
point that you're making?

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:39.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:40.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:44.376 align:middle line:84%
So that's the
intuition that we have,

00:25:44.376 --> 00:25:48.450 align:middle line:84%
that it's possible to have
feelings about sentences

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:51.790 align:middle line:84%
of the form, well, I don't know
what this is supposed to mean,

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:54.270 align:middle line:84%
but the parts are all
in the right place.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:57.330 align:middle line:84%
We are in the syntax
part of this class.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:00.180 align:middle line:84%
It's all about parts
being in the right places.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:01.990 align:middle line:84%
We will eventually do
the semantics part,

00:26:01.990 --> 00:26:03.415 align:middle line:90%
which is about meaning.

00:26:03.415 --> 00:26:05.790 align:middle line:84%
But the point is that it's
possible to study these things

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:07.660 align:middle line:90%
independently of each other.

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:10.680 align:middle line:90%
So completely independently.

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:14.340 align:middle line:84%
So when I was showing
you "unlockable,"

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:16.590 align:middle line:84%
what we were really
doing was morphology,

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.240 align:middle line:84%
but we were also
talking about meaning.

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:20.640 align:middle line:84%
We were interested
in the fact that word

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:21.820 align:middle line:90%
had two different meanings.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:25.000 align:middle line:84%
We talked about the fact that
it meant different things.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:26.880 align:middle line:84%
So we get to use semantics
as kind of a probe

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:28.600 align:middle line:84%
into what's happening
in morphology.

00:26:28.600 --> 00:26:30.930 align:middle line:84%
We'll do things
like that in syntax.

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:33.270 align:middle line:84%
But our feelings about
whether sentences

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:35.790 align:middle line:84%
are grammatical or not,
or acceptable or not,

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:38.170 align:middle line:84%
are separable from our
feelings about what they mean.

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:39.420 align:middle line:90%
That's the point, if anything.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:45.920 align:middle line:90%


00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:50.087 align:middle line:84%
The reverse, there are sentences
that are ungrammatical, but--

00:26:50.087 --> 00:26:51.670 align:middle line:84%
that are meaningless,
but grammatical,

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:54.040 align:middle line:84%
like "Colorless green
ideas sleep furiously."

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:56.950 align:middle line:84%
On the flip side,
there are sentences

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:59.213 align:middle line:84%
that are meaningful,
but ungrammatical,

00:26:59.213 --> 00:27:01.630 align:middle line:84%
the sentences where it's very
clear what they should mean,

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:04.010 align:middle line:84%
but you just can't
say it that way.

00:27:04.010 --> 00:27:08.450 align:middle line:84%
Here's a quadruple of examples
that's meant to show you that.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:11.110 align:middle line:84%
So you can say, "Put the
sweater on," you can say,

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:12.940 align:middle line:84%
"I put on the
sweater," you can say,

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:17.050 align:middle line:84%
"I put it on," you cannot
say, "I put on it."

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:18.820 align:middle line:84%
The meaning is not
the problem here.

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:22.060 align:middle line:84%
It's clear what "I
put on it" would mean.

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:24.970 align:middle line:84%
But there are facts about
how English pronouns work

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:27.550 align:middle line:84%
and how English
particles work that mean

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:29.530 align:middle line:90%
that you don't get to say that.

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:31.280 align:middle line:90%
It's not a meaning thing.

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:32.890 align:middle line:90%
It's something about syntax.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.830 align:middle line:84%
It's something about how
these parts get to combine.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:39.220 align:middle line:84%
You want to try to
understand that.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:40.930 align:middle line:84%
The only point of
these few slides

00:27:40.930 --> 00:27:44.080 align:middle line:84%
has been it's possible to
study syntax independently

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:46.900 align:middle line:84%
of meaning, where by
independently, I just

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:49.150 align:middle line:84%
mean the facts of
syntax don't just

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:50.770 align:middle line:90%
reduce to facts about meaning.

00:27:50.770 --> 00:27:52.420 align:middle line:84%
They also don't
just reduce to facts

00:27:52.420 --> 00:27:54.372 align:middle line:90%
about what you've heard before.

00:27:54.372 --> 00:27:56.080 align:middle line:84%
That's what I've been
trying to show you.

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:59.790 align:middle line:90%


00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:02.180 align:middle line:84%
Here's another thing you
might think about what's wrong

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:04.190 align:middle line:90%
with "We're class going to."

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:06.380 align:middle line:90%
It ends in a preposition.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:09.020 align:middle line:84%
Where any of you taught
in school you must not end

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:11.500 align:middle line:90%
sentences with prepositions?

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:12.400 align:middle line:90%
Some of you were--

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:13.380 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:14.070 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:28:14.070 --> 00:28:14.880 align:middle line:90%
Some of you were not.

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:15.640 align:middle line:90%
That's kind of interesting.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:16.308 align:middle line:90%
I was.

00:28:16.308 --> 00:28:18.100 align:middle line:84%
I was beaten by English
teachers for ending

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:18.960 align:middle line:90%
a sentence-- that's not true.

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:19.690 align:middle line:90%
I was not beaten.

00:28:19.690 --> 00:28:22.050 align:middle line:84%
I was spoken to harshly
by English teachers

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:24.270 align:middle line:84%
for ending sentences
with prepositions.

00:28:24.270 --> 00:28:26.730 align:middle line:84%
Do you know why we were
told not to end sentences

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:28.518 align:middle line:90%
with prepositions?

00:28:28.518 --> 00:28:30.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's reductive?

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:37.460 align:middle line:84%
Because I guess-- because
where are we going to

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:40.955 align:middle line:90%
could just be a [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:40.955 --> 00:28:42.830 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So
there are some cases like

00:28:42.830 --> 00:28:47.180 align:middle line:84%
that but look there are also
cases like, "Who are you

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:52.760 align:middle line:90%
talking to" where--

00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:55.610 align:middle line:84%
sorry, where-- who
am I apologizing to?

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:58.280 align:middle line:90%
The chalk, I guess.

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:02.450 align:middle line:84%
"Who are you talking to"
where, without the "to,"

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:04.850 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't have to be about
redundancy, I don't think.

00:29:04.850 --> 00:29:07.110 align:middle line:84%
Is this a sentence that
you've ever heard anyone say?

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:07.910 align:middle line:90%
Is this OK?

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:10.940 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, this is, I think,
pretty good English.

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:13.220 align:middle line:84%
So when our English
teachers were telling us

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:16.010 align:middle line:84%
not to end sentences
with prepositions,

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:18.695 align:middle line:84%
we should have asked them,
what are you talking about?

00:29:18.695 --> 00:29:22.200 align:middle line:90%


00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:25.490 align:middle line:84%
Because the fact is
that English speakers

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:28.400 align:middle line:84%
end sentences with
prepositions every day.

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:32.150 align:middle line:84%
Do you know why your teacher
told you not to do that?

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:32.930 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:35.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe it's because
the object isn't clear?

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.810 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Well-- but is it?

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:39.550 align:middle line:90%
I mean, it's kind of--

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:41.900 align:middle line:84%
if I ask you, who
are you talking to?

00:29:41.900 --> 00:29:43.900 align:middle line:84%
There's a sense in which
the object isn't clear.

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.360 align:middle line:84%
I'm asking you who
the object is, right?

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:50.558 align:middle line:84%
So it shouldn't be any
harder than, who are you--

00:29:50.558 --> 00:29:51.850 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Who are you talking--

00:29:51.850 --> 00:29:54.500 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
--describing, yeah.

00:29:54.500 --> 00:29:55.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:58.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it because
the "who" is supposed

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:02.450 align:middle line:84%
to be the direct object of "you
are talking to" this person,

00:30:02.450 --> 00:30:04.460 align:middle line:84%
so it's like, "to
whom are you talking"?

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:06.138 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So yeah.

00:30:06.138 --> 00:30:07.930 align:middle line:84%
What your English
teacher wanted you to say

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:09.222 align:middle line:90%
was, "To whom are you talking?"

00:30:09.222 --> 00:30:15.700 align:middle line:90%


00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:21.600 align:middle line:84%
which many English speakers
are capable of saying, possibly

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:25.470 align:middle line:84%
because their English teachers
frightened them into it.

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:27.483 align:middle line:84%
But it's not my go-to
way to say this.

00:30:27.483 --> 00:30:28.650 align:middle line:90%
I don't know about you guys.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:32.280 align:middle line:90%


00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.950 align:middle line:84%
So here, "who,"
the question word,

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:36.750 align:middle line:84%
is up here at the
beginning by itself.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:40.560 align:middle line:84%
Here, "to whom" is part of this
phrase that's at the beginning.

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:46.020 align:middle line:84%
But question, why does "to"
have to come along, according

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:47.400 align:middle line:90%
to your English teacher?

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:47.910 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:48.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this
another example

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:50.280 align:middle line:84%
because that's how
it's done in Latin?

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:50.535 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:30:50.535 --> 00:30:51.415 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:51.415 --> 00:30:52.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:52.962 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:30:52.962 --> 00:30:54.420 align:middle line:84%
Your English teacher
told you to do

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:59.160 align:middle line:84%
that because Latin, actually,
among many other languages,

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.390 align:middle line:90%
doesn't allow you to do this.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:03.750 align:middle line:90%
You have to do this.

00:31:03.750 --> 00:31:06.780 align:middle line:84%
English is quite rare in
being able to do this.

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:09.870 align:middle line:84%
Most of the languages
of the world can't.

00:31:09.870 --> 00:31:14.100 align:middle line:84%
And some time in the
15th, 16th century,

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:17.280 align:middle line:84%
a number of grammarians
decided that English

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.830 align:middle line:84%
would be way cooler if
it were more like Latin,

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:23.400 align:middle line:84%
and so they began
declaring that it was.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:26.010 align:middle line:84%
And that's why your
English teacher told you

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:30.300 align:middle line:84%
that you can't say this,
which is ridiculous.

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:32.610 align:middle line:84%
We should be proud of the
fact that we can say this,

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:33.960 align:middle line:90%
because, as I say, it's rare.

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:35.250 align:middle line:90%
Most languages can't do this.

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:38.130 align:middle line:84%
We should have this on our
flag or something like that.

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:41.430 align:middle line:84%
There should be a stranded
preposition, a preposition

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:43.620 align:middle line:90%
at the end of the sentence.

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:48.540 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, no reason English
has to be like Latin.

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:51.210 align:middle line:84%
Or like I say, a
zillion other languages.

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:54.570 align:middle line:84%
Most of the languages of
Europe, French, or German,

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:57.240 align:middle line:84%
or Italian, or whatever,
you can't leave prepositions

00:31:57.240 --> 00:31:58.927 align:middle line:90%
at the ends of sentences.

00:31:58.927 --> 00:32:01.260 align:middle line:84%
But those teachers don't have
to tell their children not

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.540 align:middle line:84%
to say things like,
who are you talking to,

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.270 align:middle line:84%
because they literally
can't, and none of those kids

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:07.600 align:middle line:90%
would ever do that.

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:08.835 align:middle line:90%
But in English, we can.

00:32:08.835 --> 00:32:09.960 align:middle line:90%
We should be proud of that.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:13.080 align:middle line:90%


00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:16.710 align:middle line:84%
So that's not the problem
with "We're class going to."

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:19.650 align:middle line:84%
It does end in a preposition,
but many perfectly fine

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:22.920 align:middle line:84%
English sentences
end in prepositions.

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:24.930 align:middle line:84%
There's a distinction--
yeah, there's

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.730 align:middle line:84%
the example "Who
are you talking to?"

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:28.147 align:middle line:84%
There's a distinction
that's worth

00:32:28.147 --> 00:32:32.490 align:middle line:84%
drawing between what's called
prescriptive and descriptive

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:33.910 align:middle line:90%
studies of grammar.

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:36.450 align:middle line:84%
So what we are
doing in this class

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:39.270 align:middle line:84%
is trying to figure out
what people actually say,

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:43.470 align:middle line:84%
what the rules are for putting
sentences together in English.

00:32:43.470 --> 00:32:47.160 align:middle line:84%
There are other kinds of things
that people say about how

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:49.240 align:middle line:90%
English should be spoken.

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:52.140 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to talk about
that stuff, except to mock it

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:53.880 align:middle line:90%
the way I did just now.

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.703 align:middle line:84%
So prescriptive grammar
is the study of rules

00:32:57.703 --> 00:32:59.370 align:middle line:84%
that your teachers
might have taught you

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:01.860 align:middle line:84%
in school about how to
speak, some of which,

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:04.200 align:middle line:84%
just to stop mocking
it for a second,

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:06.660 align:middle line:84%
your teachers might
have tried to tell you

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:09.240 align:middle line:84%
things that would
genuinely improve

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:12.480 align:middle line:84%
the quality of your writing,
like get rid of ambiguities

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:13.290 align:middle line:90%
of various kinds.

00:33:13.290 --> 00:33:17.100 align:middle line:84%
So if you're now mentally
composing nasty messages

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:18.970 align:middle line:84%
to your English teachers,
don't send them.

00:33:18.970 --> 00:33:20.633 align:middle line:84%
They had your best
interests at heart,

00:33:20.633 --> 00:33:22.050 align:middle line:84%
and they probably
taught you a lot

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:23.430 align:middle line:90%
of things that were valuable.

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:25.140 align:middle line:84%
But they also taught
you some things

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:29.195 align:middle line:84%
that became popular around
the 15th, 16th century

00:33:29.195 --> 00:33:31.320 align:middle line:84%
because people thought that
English would be better

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:32.760 align:middle line:90%
if it were more like Latin.

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:36.300 align:middle line:84%
And so we're not going to
try to improve your writing

00:33:36.300 --> 00:33:38.790 align:middle line:84%
in this class, except
insofar as the writing

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:41.160 align:middle line:90%
advisors can do that.

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:44.340 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to be talking
about prescriptive studies

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:45.570 align:middle line:90%
of English grammar.

00:33:45.570 --> 00:33:47.250 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to talk
about how you should speak,

00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:48.210 align:middle line:90%
or how you should write.

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:49.890 align:middle line:84%
We're just going to
talk about how you do.

00:33:49.890 --> 00:33:52.432 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to be a study
of descriptive grammar and not

00:33:52.432 --> 00:33:53.760 align:middle line:90%
prescriptive.

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:55.318 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:55.318 --> 00:33:56.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So for
that second sentence

00:33:56.860 --> 00:33:59.110 align:middle line:84%
on the board, "What
are you talking about?"

00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:00.360 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, this one?

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:00.886 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:34:00.886 --> 00:34:05.105 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How would you answer
that such that something

00:34:05.105 --> 00:34:08.030 align:middle line:84%
[? throws ?] the word
[? around. ?] Because "About

00:34:08.030 --> 00:34:09.806 align:middle line:90%
what are you talking?"

00:34:09.806 --> 00:34:10.659 align:middle line:90%
is--

00:34:10.659 --> 00:34:13.326 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I can't do that
in English, "About what are you

00:34:13.326 --> 00:34:15.432 align:middle line:90%
talking?"

00:34:15.432 --> 00:34:18.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That sounds
wrong, actually.

00:34:18.550 --> 00:34:21.480 align:middle line:84%
Whereas "To whom
are you talking?"

00:34:21.480 --> 00:34:23.572 align:middle line:84%
sounds fancy and
snobbish, but still right.

00:34:23.572 --> 00:34:24.989 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:27.389 align:middle line:84%
No, you're raising an
interesting distinction.

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:30.840 align:middle line:84%
So in many languages, most
languages, including Latin, you

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:32.610 align:middle line:84%
have to say, "About
what are you talking?"

00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:34.568 align:middle line:84%
and you have to say, "To
whom are you talking?"

00:34:34.568 --> 00:34:37.215 align:middle line:84%
So these are languages in which
you never leave a preposition

00:34:37.215 --> 00:34:38.340 align:middle line:90%
at the end of the sentence.

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:40.594 align:middle line:84%
You always bring it along
with the question word

00:34:40.594 --> 00:34:41.969 align:middle line:84%
to the beginning
of the sentence.

00:34:41.969 --> 00:34:43.886 align:middle line:84%
But you're raising a
really interesting point.

00:34:43.886 --> 00:34:45.719 align:middle line:84%
In English, there's
this distinction

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:50.280 align:middle line:84%
between the examples where
leaving a preposition behind

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:52.840 align:middle line:84%
is what you prefer,
"Who are you talking to"

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:55.110 align:middle line:84%
But you can kind of say,
"To whom are you talking?"

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:58.380 align:middle line:84%
And others, were "About
what are you talking?"

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:01.050 align:middle line:84%
Do other people have this
intuition, that "About what are

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:04.080 align:middle line:84%
you talking?" is worse than
"To whom are you talking?"

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:07.560 align:middle line:84%
I have that feeling,
too, I think.

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:10.300 align:middle line:90%
There are other examples.

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:12.885 align:middle line:84%
There are examples that
are really quite bad.

00:35:12.885 --> 00:35:18.630 align:middle line:90%


00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:20.130 align:middle line:84%
And other examples
which get better.

00:35:20.130 --> 00:35:32.445 align:middle line:84%
So things like "We left
despite her warnings."

00:35:32.445 --> 00:35:36.540 align:middle line:90%


00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.730 align:middle line:84%
And then consider two
kinds of questions

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:39.940 align:middle line:90%
you could ask about that.

00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:41.325 align:middle line:90%
"What did you leave despite?"

00:35:41.325 --> 00:35:49.570 align:middle line:90%


00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:51.730 align:middle line:84%
And "Despite what
did you leave?"

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:59.890 align:middle line:90%


00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:02.490 align:middle line:84%
Is either of those
acceptable at all?

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:03.810 align:middle line:90%
"What did you leave despite?"

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:05.280 align:middle line:84%
and "Despite what
did you leave?"

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:08.030 align:middle line:84%
Who prefers "What did
you leave despite?"

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:10.220 align:middle line:84%
Who prefers "Despite
what did you leave?"

00:36:10.220 --> 00:36:13.610 align:middle line:84%
Who would do anything to avoid
saying either of these things?

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:14.670 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:15.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:20.730 align:middle line:84%
So here's another example of
one sentence that's quite bad

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:24.040 align:middle line:84%
and another sentence
that's only bad-ish.

00:36:24.040 --> 00:36:26.690 align:middle line:84%
You might want to try to
understand what's going on.

00:36:26.690 --> 00:36:28.850 align:middle line:84%
So there's a fruitful
area of research here.

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:30.380 align:middle line:90%
OK, English doesn't--

00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:32.060 align:middle line:84%
English is happy to
leave prepositions

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:33.200 align:middle line:90%
at the ends of sentences.

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:36.000 align:middle line:84%
But in which cases is it
happy to do the Latin thing?

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:37.870 align:middle line:84%
And in some cases, it's
happier than others.

00:36:37.870 --> 00:36:39.245 align:middle line:84%
It's interesting
to try to study.

00:36:39.245 --> 00:36:47.650 align:middle line:90%


00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:49.190 align:middle line:90%
Another distinction to make.

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:50.950 align:middle line:84%
So we've drawn this
distinction now

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:54.640 align:middle line:84%
between meaningless
on the one hand

00:36:54.640 --> 00:36:56.320 align:middle line:90%
and ungrammatical on the other.

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:58.750 align:middle line:84%
That sentence can be both
meaningless and ungrammatical,

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:00.708 align:middle line:84%
but it can also be
meaningless and grammatical,

00:37:00.708 --> 00:37:04.202 align:middle line:84%
like "Colorless green
ideas sleep furiously."

00:37:04.202 --> 00:37:05.410 align:middle line:90%
We've drawn that distinction.

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:09.280 align:middle line:84%
We've drawn a distinction
between prescriptive and

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:10.378 align:middle line:90%
descriptive statements.

00:37:10.378 --> 00:37:11.920 align:middle line:84%
So prescriptive
statements are things

00:37:11.920 --> 00:37:15.220 align:middle line:84%
like "Don't end sentences with
prepositions," which are--

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:17.290 align:middle line:90%
how shall I say this?-- false.

00:37:17.290 --> 00:37:19.870 align:middle line:84%
Not a good description of what
English speakers actually do.

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:22.085 align:middle line:90%
They are aspirations.

00:37:22.085 --> 00:37:23.210 align:middle line:90%
We're not going to do that.

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:27.460 align:middle line:84%
We're just going to study
what English speakers do.

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:29.080 align:middle line:84%
So here's another
useful distinction.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:33.430 align:middle line:84%
It's sometimes called
competence versus performance.

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:37.130 align:middle line:84%
Imagine that I am standing
up here talking to you.

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:40.220 align:middle line:84%
So far, this should
be easy to imagine.

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:41.410 align:middle line:90%
Except I'm not standing.

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:45.370 align:middle line:84%
Imagine that I'm standing up
here talking to you and I say,

00:37:45.370 --> 00:37:49.107 align:middle line:84%
"This is the--" and
then I Inhale a fly.

00:37:49.107 --> 00:37:51.190 align:middle line:84%
So imagine that-- oh, I
don't have to wear a mask.

00:37:51.190 --> 00:37:53.710 align:middle line:90%
I keep forgetting that.

00:37:53.710 --> 00:37:56.380 align:middle line:84%
Imagine that I take off my
mask and I Inhale a fly.

00:37:56.380 --> 00:37:58.780 align:middle line:84%
So I say, "This is
the--" and then I stop.

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:01.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm like [IMITATES COUGHING]
and I stop right there.

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:05.290 align:middle line:84%
And then imagine
that this experience

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:12.070 align:middle line:84%
is so traumatizing for me
and also for the fly, that I

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:14.650 align:middle line:84%
just I never complete
that sentence.

00:38:14.650 --> 00:38:17.710 align:middle line:84%
So I say, "This is
the--" and then I stop.

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:21.400 align:middle line:90%


00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:24.332 align:middle line:84%
There are two attitudes
that we could have to me

00:38:24.332 --> 00:38:25.540 align:middle line:90%
having uttered that sentence.

00:38:25.540 --> 00:38:26.790 align:middle line:90%
That's the sentence I uttered.

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:30.250 align:middle line:90%
"This is the cough, ugh, puh."

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:31.630 align:middle line:90%
That's something I said.

00:38:31.630 --> 00:38:35.143 align:middle line:84%
And I'm a native
speaker of English.

00:38:35.143 --> 00:38:37.060 align:middle line:84%
So there are two kinds
of things we could say.

00:38:37.060 --> 00:38:39.760 align:middle line:84%
One would be to say,
we're developing

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:42.130 align:middle line:84%
a theory of all of
the kinds of sentences

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:44.200 align:middle line:84%
that Native English
speakers can say.

00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:46.150 align:middle line:90%
And that was one.

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:48.910 align:middle line:84%
"This is the cough
hack splutter."

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:51.310 align:middle line:84%
And so we want a
theory that allows

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:52.960 align:middle line:84%
that to be a
sentence of English.

00:38:52.960 --> 00:38:55.890 align:middle line:84%
That's one kind of
thing we could say.

00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:58.500 align:middle line:84%
This is one of those moments
that happens a lot in classes

00:38:58.500 --> 00:38:59.880 align:middle line:84%
where the professor
says, "Here's

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:02.520 align:middle line:84%
one thing we could say,"
and then describes something

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:04.500 align:middle line:90%
completely ridiculous.

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:06.157 align:middle line:90%
You could do that.

00:39:06.157 --> 00:39:07.740 align:middle line:84%
But here's another
thing we could say.

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:10.355 align:middle line:84%
We could say, no, look, if
we're developing a theory of all

00:39:10.355 --> 00:39:12.480 align:middle line:84%
of the things that native
English speakers can say,

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:14.105 align:middle line:84%
native speakers of
any language at all,

00:39:14.105 --> 00:39:16.860 align:middle line:84%
but we're going to
start with English,

00:39:16.860 --> 00:39:19.830 align:middle line:84%
what we want is not a
theory that covers "This

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:21.218 align:middle line:90%
is the cough, hack, splutter."

00:39:21.218 --> 00:39:23.010 align:middle line:84%
That's not going to be
one of the sentences

00:39:23.010 --> 00:39:24.093 align:middle line:90%
we're going to try to get.

00:39:24.093 --> 00:39:26.280 align:middle line:84%
We're going to try to
get sentences like, "This

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:30.510 align:middle line:90%
is the answer" or whatever.

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:34.350 align:middle line:84%
That's going to be a sentence,
whatever it is I meant to say.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:36.660 align:middle line:84%
And then there are going
to be other things.

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:43.350 align:middle line:84%
Flies, sudden heart
attacks, and then

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:45.458 align:middle line:84%
other kinds of
things that are maybe

00:39:45.458 --> 00:39:47.250 align:middle line:84%
less clear to think
what to say about them.

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:49.020 align:middle line:90%
Flies, sure.

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:50.615 align:middle line:90%
Sudden heart attacks, yeah.

00:39:50.615 --> 00:39:51.990 align:middle line:84%
What about if I
forget what I was

00:39:51.990 --> 00:39:53.790 align:middle line:84%
going to say in the
middle of a sentence.

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:55.380 align:middle line:84%
I know that's hard to
imagine, but imagine

00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:56.580 align:middle line:90%
that I did something like that.

00:39:56.580 --> 00:39:58.622 align:middle line:84%
I'm in the middle of a
sentence, and I'm talking,

00:39:58.622 --> 00:40:01.863 align:middle line:84%
and then I just forget
where I was going, and--

00:40:01.863 --> 00:40:03.030 align:middle line:90%
where was I going with that?

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:04.980 align:middle line:90%
Who knows.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:06.580 align:middle line:90%
That could happen.

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:08.290 align:middle line:90%
So what we're going to have--

00:40:08.290 --> 00:40:10.800 align:middle line:84%
so this is a different
approach, and it's the one

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:12.630 align:middle line:84%
that you might imagine
I'm recommending.

00:40:12.630 --> 00:40:14.130 align:middle line:84%
What we're going
to have is the idea

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:16.260 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to
develop a theory of what

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.210 align:middle line:84%
English speakers
say, but we're going

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:20.610 align:middle line:84%
to imagine the kind
of English speaker

00:40:20.610 --> 00:40:24.090 align:middle line:84%
who never inhales flies, and
never forgets what they were

00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:26.640 align:middle line:84%
going to say, never has
a fatal heart attack,

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:32.480 align:middle line:84%
and only speaks in completely
grammatical sentences.

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:36.710 align:middle line:84%
There might not be any
speakers like that.

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:39.230 align:middle line:84%
If you have ever looked at
a transcript of somebody

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:44.210 align:middle line:84%
talking, there aren't
any sentences in there,

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:47.510 align:middle line:84%
unless the person
is reading a text,

00:40:47.510 --> 00:40:50.930 align:middle line:84%
or unless the person is
Noam Chomsky, I have to say.

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:53.510 align:middle line:84%
It was quite weird
reading transcripts

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:56.030 align:middle line:84%
of Noam Chomsky talking, or
listening to Noam Chomsky talk,

00:40:56.030 --> 00:40:59.420 align:middle line:84%
because, in fact, he talks in
complete sentences, paragraphs.

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:01.460 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of astonishing.

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:03.860 align:middle line:84%
Normally, if you look at
the transcript of somebody

00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:05.397 align:middle line:90%
talking--

00:41:05.397 --> 00:41:07.730 align:middle line:84%
speaking of Noam Chomsky,
I've often heard him say this.

00:41:07.730 --> 00:41:10.490 align:middle line:84%
Journalists know
that the best way

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:12.920 align:middle line:84%
to make someone look
like a complete idiot is

00:41:12.920 --> 00:41:14.630 align:middle line:90%
to quote them accurately.

00:41:14.630 --> 00:41:16.250 align:middle line:84%
Just to write down
exactly what they

00:41:16.250 --> 00:41:19.172 align:middle line:84%
said, because they'll say "um"
and "uh" and they'll stop,

00:41:19.172 --> 00:41:21.380 align:middle line:84%
and they'll pause, and
they'll change what they said,

00:41:21.380 --> 00:41:22.730 align:middle line:90%
and they'll inhale flies.

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:27.590 align:middle line:84%
Things will happen such that
their sentences are not fully

00:41:27.590 --> 00:41:29.870 align:middle line:90%
grammatical sentences.

00:41:29.870 --> 00:41:32.900 align:middle line:84%
What journalists, in fact, do
is to clean up all that stuff

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:34.520 align:middle line:84%
so that people
sounded like they were

00:41:34.520 --> 00:41:36.530 align:middle line:90%
talking in complete sentences.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:38.570 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
develop a theory of what

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:40.100 align:middle line:84%
English speakers
say, but it's going

00:41:40.100 --> 00:41:44.900 align:middle line:84%
to be a theory that's
divorced from reality

00:41:44.900 --> 00:41:46.400 align:middle line:90%
to a certain extent.

00:41:46.400 --> 00:41:49.010 align:middle line:84%
We're going to imagine
what people would be like

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:51.650 align:middle line:84%
if there were no
distractions, and no flies,

00:41:51.650 --> 00:41:55.580 align:middle line:84%
and no sudden
homicides, no falling

00:41:55.580 --> 00:41:59.490 align:middle line:84%
asleep in the middle of your
sentences, all of that stuff.

00:41:59.490 --> 00:42:03.230 align:middle line:84%
So the distinction here is
competence versus performance.

00:42:03.230 --> 00:42:05.240 align:middle line:84%
We're imagining a
speaker who's kind

00:42:05.240 --> 00:42:10.775 align:middle line:84%
of like a frictionless
plane, that there

00:42:10.775 --> 00:42:12.590 align:middle line:84%
are various kinds
of complications,

00:42:12.590 --> 00:42:14.720 align:middle line:84%
and there's no air
resistance or whatever else.

00:42:14.720 --> 00:42:17.877 align:middle line:84%
Various kinds of
complications don't arise.

00:42:17.877 --> 00:42:20.210 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to be talking
about speakers' competence,

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:23.120 align:middle line:84%
what they would do if
there were no distractions

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:24.600 align:middle line:90%
and no problems.

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:26.090 align:middle line:90%
There's also performance.

00:42:26.090 --> 00:42:28.670 align:middle line:84%
That's the study of
what people actually do.

00:42:28.670 --> 00:42:30.500 align:middle line:84%
And we want to study
that, sure, but we're

00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:32.810 align:middle line:84%
going to develop a theory
of competence on the theory

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:35.690 align:middle line:84%
that it'll be
simpler in the hope

00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:39.562 align:middle line:84%
that by abstracting away from
various kinds of complications,

00:42:39.562 --> 00:42:41.520 align:middle line:84%
we'll get a clearer
picture of what's going on.

00:42:41.520 --> 00:42:42.290 align:middle line:90%
Does that makes sense?

00:42:42.290 --> 00:42:43.790 align:middle line:84%
That's how we're
going to do syntax.

00:42:43.790 --> 00:42:48.950 align:middle line:90%


00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:53.660 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, "It's raining" is a
possible sentence of English.

00:42:53.660 --> 00:42:55.220 align:middle line:84%
"John thinks that
it's raining" is

00:42:55.220 --> 00:42:56.730 align:middle line:90%
a possible sentence of English.

00:42:56.730 --> 00:42:58.730 align:middle line:84%
"Mary thinks that John
thinks that it's raining"

00:42:58.730 --> 00:43:00.560 align:middle line:84%
is a possible
sentence of English.

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:04.130 align:middle line:84%
In fact, for any
sentence in English,

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:07.640 align:middle line:84%
it's always possible to
create a longer sentence,

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:09.320 align:middle line:90%
so take any sentence, S.

00:43:09.320 --> 00:43:13.610 align:middle line:84%
Here's a recipe for another
sentence of English.

00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:21.230 align:middle line:84%
You can always say,
"She thinks that S,"

00:43:21.230 --> 00:43:24.230 align:middle line:84%
where she maybe refers
to different people

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:25.530 align:middle line:90%
in every clause.

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:27.628 align:middle line:90%
So you can say, "It's raining."

00:43:27.628 --> 00:43:28.920 align:middle line:90%
"She thinks that it's raining."

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:30.510 align:middle line:84%
"She thinks that she
thinks that it's raining."

00:43:30.510 --> 00:43:33.093 align:middle line:84%
"She thinks that she thinks that
she thinks that it's raining"

00:43:33.093 --> 00:43:35.510 align:middle line:90%
can keep going arbitrarily long.

00:43:35.510 --> 00:43:42.350 align:middle line:84%
There is no bound on the
length of English sentences.

00:43:42.350 --> 00:43:44.240 align:middle line:84%
When we say it that
way, you can tell

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:48.260 align:middle line:84%
that I am talking about
competence because no matter

00:43:48.260 --> 00:43:50.720 align:middle line:84%
how many recordings of English
speakers you go through,

00:43:50.720 --> 00:43:53.780 align:middle line:84%
you will never find an
infinitely long sentence.

00:43:53.780 --> 00:43:56.390 align:middle line:84%
Nobody actually
says these things.

00:43:56.390 --> 00:44:01.130 align:middle line:84%
But the reason nobody says
an infinitely long sentence,

00:44:01.130 --> 00:44:02.390 align:middle line:90%
the idea is going to be--

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:04.890 align:middle line:90%
That isn't a fact about grammar.

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:06.440 align:middle line:90%
It's a fact about life.

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:09.410 align:middle line:84%
And we don't care about
life in this class.

00:44:09.410 --> 00:44:12.292 align:middle line:84%
The fact that if I
were to start saying,

00:44:12.292 --> 00:44:14.750 align:middle line:84%
"She thinks that she thinks
that she thinks that she thinks

00:44:14.750 --> 00:44:16.910 align:middle line:84%
that she thinks that she thinks
that she thinks that she thinks

00:44:16.910 --> 00:44:19.118 align:middle line:84%
that it's raining," then
eventually people would stop

00:44:19.118 --> 00:44:21.380 align:middle line:84%
listening to me, or I
would run out of breath,

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:24.020 align:middle line:84%
or I would need to take a
break to eat, or I would die--

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:26.660 align:middle line:84%
there are various reasons
that I will eventually

00:44:26.660 --> 00:44:29.870 align:middle line:84%
stop uttering my
infinitely long sentence,

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:32.237 align:middle line:84%
but we're not going to
have a grammar that says--

00:44:32.237 --> 00:44:34.820 align:middle line:84%
we're not going to try to find
out what's the longest sentence

00:44:34.820 --> 00:44:37.970 align:middle line:84%
anybody ever uttered
and try to get that fact

00:44:37.970 --> 00:44:40.218 align:middle line:84%
to be a fact that we want
our grammar of English,

00:44:40.218 --> 00:44:42.260 align:middle line:84%
our theory of the possible
sentences of English--

00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:44.930 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to
try to predict that.

00:44:44.930 --> 00:44:46.430 align:middle line:84%
What we're going
to have is a theory

00:44:46.430 --> 00:44:49.670 align:middle line:84%
that says English sentences
can be arbitrarily long.

00:44:49.670 --> 00:44:51.733 align:middle line:84%
And then, yeah,
eventually people die,

00:44:51.733 --> 00:44:53.150 align:middle line:84%
and so nobody ever
says a sentence

00:44:53.150 --> 00:44:54.108 align:middle line:90%
that's infinitely long.

00:44:54.108 --> 00:44:55.220 align:middle line:90%
But that's about death.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:56.803 align:middle line:84%
That's not about--
and we're not going

00:44:56.803 --> 00:44:59.220 align:middle line:84%
to talk about death in this
class, except when we do.

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:00.270 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:01.468 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:45:01.468 --> 00:45:03.260 align:middle line:84%
So that's another move
we're going to make,

00:45:03.260 --> 00:45:05.792 align:middle line:84%
another instance of us
caring about the difference

00:45:05.792 --> 00:45:07.250 align:middle line:84%
between competence
and performance.

00:45:07.250 --> 00:45:10.250 align:middle line:84%
Nobody ever performs an
infinitely long sentence,

00:45:10.250 --> 00:45:13.842 align:middle line:84%
but we're competent
to produce them.

00:45:13.842 --> 00:45:15.800 align:middle line:84%
So enough talking about
what we're going to do.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:17.330 align:middle line:90%
Let's begin doing it.

00:45:17.330 --> 00:45:21.170 align:middle line:84%
Questions before I
begin doing some syntax?

00:45:21.170 --> 00:45:23.600 align:middle line:84%
Here's the sentence, "I
will find the red book."

00:45:23.600 --> 00:45:25.220 align:middle line:90%
grammatical sentence.

00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:25.775 align:middle line:90%
Acceptable.

00:45:25.775 --> 00:45:27.650 align:middle line:84%
It's clear what it means,
although we've just

00:45:27.650 --> 00:45:29.692 align:middle line:84%
said it doesn't matter
whether it means anything.

00:45:29.692 --> 00:45:31.880 align:middle line:90%


00:45:31.880 --> 00:45:35.930 align:middle line:84%
I said early on
we're going to want

00:45:35.930 --> 00:45:40.820 align:middle line:84%
to have a way of saying
which parts of this sentence

00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:45.320 align:middle line:84%
were put together as units,
like with "unlockable."

00:45:45.320 --> 00:45:47.780 align:middle line:84%
We wanted to be able
to say "unlockable"

00:45:47.780 --> 00:45:50.870 align:middle line:84%
is ambiguous because it can
consist of a unit "unlock"

00:45:50.870 --> 00:45:53.513 align:middle line:84%
to which you've added
"-able," or a unit "lockable"

00:45:53.513 --> 00:45:54.680 align:middle line:90%
to which you've added "un-."

00:45:54.680 --> 00:45:57.003 align:middle line:84%
That's what that
ambiguity comes from.

00:45:57.003 --> 00:45:59.420 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to do a similar
kind of thing with syntax.

00:45:59.420 --> 00:46:01.180 align:middle line:84%
We're going to look
for these units.

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:02.930 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going
to find is that there

00:46:02.930 --> 00:46:05.960 align:middle line:84%
are various phenomena
that care about

00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:09.440 align:middle line:84%
whether something is a unit
in that tree, a single blob

00:46:09.440 --> 00:46:11.580 align:middle line:90%
of structure.

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:14.250 align:middle line:84%
So in a sentence like, "I will
find the red book" for example,

00:46:14.250 --> 00:46:17.070 align:middle line:84%
we'll see that syntax
treats that string,

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:20.130 align:middle line:90%
"The red book" as a unit.

00:46:20.130 --> 00:46:22.560 align:middle line:84%
There are various phenomena
that care about that.

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:23.990 align:middle line:84%
So one of them is
what's sometimes

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:25.340 align:middle line:90%
called topicalization.

00:46:25.340 --> 00:46:30.920 align:middle line:84%
It's possible to say things
like "The red book I will find."

00:46:30.920 --> 00:46:33.050 align:middle line:84%
For me at least, it's
easiest to say things

00:46:33.050 --> 00:46:35.150 align:middle line:84%
like that if I follow it
up with "The blue book,

00:46:35.150 --> 00:46:37.820 align:middle line:90%
I will leave right where it is."

00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:42.560 align:middle line:84%
It's OK to say things like
"The red book I will find."

00:46:42.560 --> 00:46:46.670 align:middle line:84%
It's OK to take a substring
like that, "the red book"

00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:51.390 align:middle line:84%
and put it together with another
similar substring conjoined

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:53.390 align:middle line:84%
with the word "and." so
you can say things like,

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:56.990 align:middle line:84%
"I will find the red book
and the blue pencils."

00:46:56.990 --> 00:47:03.060 align:middle line:84%
It is OK to use "the red
book" as a possible answer

00:47:03.060 --> 00:47:04.180 align:middle line:90%
to a question.

00:47:04.180 --> 00:47:08.070 align:middle line:84%
This is like the stuff we were
saying before about things you

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:10.620 align:middle line:90%
can say if you're astonished.

00:47:10.620 --> 00:47:15.420 align:middle line:84%
So you can say "the red book"
as, basically, a sentence

00:47:15.420 --> 00:47:16.720 align:middle line:90%
under the right circumstances.

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:18.928 align:middle line:84%
For example, if somebody
has just asked you what will

00:47:18.928 --> 00:47:20.580 align:middle line:90%
you find.

00:47:20.580 --> 00:47:22.650 align:middle line:84%
These are all ways in
which "the red book"

00:47:22.650 --> 00:47:26.790 align:middle line:84%
is treated as a single object
that syntax gets to refer to.

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:29.370 align:middle line:84%
I hope I was smart enough to
contrast it with something

00:47:29.370 --> 00:47:30.850 align:middle line:90%
else on the next slide.

00:47:30.850 --> 00:47:31.350 align:middle line:90%
Yes, I was.

00:47:31.350 --> 00:47:34.890 align:middle line:84%
But let me give
you one other test.

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:37.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, this is a test.

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:40.780 align:middle line:84%
I can say things like "what
I will find is the red book."

00:47:40.780 --> 00:47:43.260 align:middle line:84%
So I can rearrange the words
of the sentence in a way such

00:47:43.260 --> 00:47:46.000 align:middle line:84%
that there is a word,
"is" before that string

00:47:46.000 --> 00:47:46.950 align:middle line:90%
"the red book."

00:47:46.950 --> 00:47:50.650 align:middle line:84%
It puts a special kind of
emphasis on "the red book."

00:47:50.650 --> 00:47:52.470 align:middle line:90%
It's called clefting.

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:54.690 align:middle line:84%
Contrast that with--
so this is not just

00:47:54.690 --> 00:47:57.880 align:middle line:84%
a property of every three-word
string in the sentence.

00:47:57.880 --> 00:48:01.560 align:middle line:84%
So "find the red," for
example, is not a constituent.

00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:02.400 align:middle line:90%
It's not a phrase.

00:48:02.400 --> 00:48:04.400 align:middle line:84%
It's not something we
need to make reference to.

00:48:04.400 --> 00:48:08.310 align:middle line:84%
So you cannot say things like
"find the red I will book."

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:10.350 align:middle line:84%
So "the red book, I will
find," "the blue book,

00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:11.700 align:middle line:90%
I will leave where it is," fine.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:17.790 align:middle line:84%
But "find the red I will book,
leave the blue I will pencils."

00:48:17.790 --> 00:48:19.050 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:48:19.050 --> 00:48:23.280 align:middle line:84%
Can't do this with just any
random three-word string.

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:25.500 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, there
is no question--

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:27.990 align:middle line:84%
there are questions to which
the answer is "The red book,"

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:29.250 align:middle line:90%
like "What will you find?"

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:31.290 align:middle line:84%
There are no questions
to which the answer is

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:36.050 align:middle line:84%
"Find the red,"
apart from "What are

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:39.590 align:middle line:84%
the third, fourth, and fifth
words of this sentence?"

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:43.640 align:middle line:84%
So put aside mental
linguistic games like that.

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:46.730 align:middle line:84%
You can't-- and there's no
question that will give you

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:47.420 align:middle line:90%
that answer.

00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:48.140 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:50.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What color
do I need to find?

00:48:50.090 --> 00:48:52.090 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "What
color do I need to find?"

00:48:52.090 --> 00:48:54.000 align:middle line:90%
"Find the red."

00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:55.540 align:middle line:90%
Really?

00:48:55.540 --> 00:48:56.470 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I see.

00:48:56.470 --> 00:49:00.210 align:middle line:84%
You mean "What color
book do I need to find?"

00:49:00.210 --> 00:49:02.390 align:middle line:90%
"Find the red."

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:04.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How [? about ?]
"Find the red one?"

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:05.390 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Find the red--

00:49:05.390 --> 00:49:06.785 align:middle line:84%
I want to say
"Find the red one."

00:49:06.785 --> 00:49:09.640 align:middle line:90%


00:49:09.640 --> 00:49:10.140 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:49:10.140 --> 00:49:13.640 align:middle line:90%


00:49:13.640 --> 00:49:18.190 align:middle line:84%
Is there a faster way
to convince you of that?

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:20.950 align:middle line:84%
Or similarly, if I
switch to my other way

00:49:20.950 --> 00:49:25.000 align:middle line:84%
of eliciting fragments of
sentences, if I tell you

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:27.700 align:middle line:84%
I will find the red
book and you're amazed,

00:49:27.700 --> 00:49:29.770 align:middle line:90%
you can say, "The red book?"

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:32.380 align:middle line:84%
But if I tell you that
I will find the red book

00:49:32.380 --> 00:49:35.890 align:middle line:84%
and you're amazed, you're not
going to say, "Find the red?"

00:49:35.890 --> 00:49:36.550 align:middle line:90%
I think.

00:49:36.550 --> 00:49:38.830 align:middle line:90%
Do you think that's true?

00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:41.380 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, I take
your point about--

00:49:41.380 --> 00:49:43.840 align:middle line:84%
to the extent that you can
use "the red" as shorthand

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:45.268 align:middle line:90%
for "the red one."

00:49:45.268 --> 00:49:47.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's red acting as
the noun, not red acting--

00:49:47.560 --> 00:49:48.190 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:49:48.190 --> 00:49:48.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:49:48.580 --> 00:49:48.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, no.

00:49:48.940 --> 00:49:50.170 align:middle line:90%
That's an interesting point.

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:52.003 align:middle line:84%
Several people have
points about that point.

00:49:52.003 --> 00:49:53.177 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:49:53.177 --> 00:49:55.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think "Find the
red" is an acceptable answer

00:49:55.510 --> 00:49:57.450 align:middle line:84%
to a question that
you're finding this--

00:49:57.450 --> 00:49:59.700 align:middle line:84%
suppose you have
this fictional--

00:49:59.700 --> 00:50:01.200 align:middle line:84%
this children's
game where there's

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:02.550 align:middle line:90%
a bunch of little tiles.

00:50:02.550 --> 00:50:04.125 align:middle line:90%
You have to find the red--

00:50:04.125 --> 00:50:05.250 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah yeah.

00:50:05.250 --> 00:50:06.270 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: --the red ones.

00:50:06.270 --> 00:50:06.540 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:08.003 align:middle line:84%
So this is like
your example where

00:50:08.003 --> 00:50:09.420 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
treat red as a noun

00:50:09.420 --> 00:50:10.628 align:middle line:90%
that you're going to go find.

00:50:10.628 --> 00:50:12.712 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess this is
kind of the same thing--

00:50:12.712 --> 00:50:14.400 align:middle line:84%
"land of the free,"
"home of the brave."

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:15.180 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh.

00:50:15.180 --> 00:50:15.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:15.680 --> 00:50:19.170 align:middle line:84%
So we have some cases
where we have things

00:50:19.170 --> 00:50:21.300 align:middle line:84%
that you certainly--
should be adjectives,

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:23.970 align:middle line:84%
that either we're getting
to use them as nouns,

00:50:23.970 --> 00:50:28.272 align:middle line:84%
or we're getting to modify
nouns that you can't hear,

00:50:28.272 --> 00:50:29.730 align:middle line:84%
however we want to
talk about that.

00:50:29.730 --> 00:50:30.438 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good point.

00:50:30.438 --> 00:50:31.950 align:middle line:90%
Good point.

00:50:31.950 --> 00:50:32.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:50:32.630 --> 00:50:33.630 align:middle line:90%
Other points about this?

00:50:33.630 --> 00:50:36.330 align:middle line:90%


00:50:36.330 --> 00:50:38.400 align:middle line:84%
So all this slide
is meant to convince

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:40.845 align:middle line:84%
you of is that "the red
book" and "find the red"

00:50:40.845 --> 00:50:41.970 align:middle line:90%
don't have the same status.

00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:44.340 align:middle line:84%
"The red book," we want
it to be a substring that

00:50:44.340 --> 00:50:47.910 align:middle line:84%
has certain privileges, can be
used for these various types

00:50:47.910 --> 00:50:51.070 align:middle line:84%
of phenomena, as opposed
to "find the red,"

00:50:51.070 --> 00:50:53.100 align:middle line:84%
which you can't do
those things with.

00:50:53.100 --> 00:50:55.950 align:middle line:84%
So it isn't just these are
phenomena that pick out

00:50:55.950 --> 00:50:58.117 align:middle line:90%
three-words substrings.

00:50:58.117 --> 00:50:59.700 align:middle line:84%
It's these are
phenomena that pick out

00:50:59.700 --> 00:51:01.232 align:middle line:84%
certain substrings
and not others

00:51:01.232 --> 00:51:02.940 align:middle line:84%
of the sentence,
certain strings of words

00:51:02.940 --> 00:51:05.550 align:middle line:84%
and not others of the
sentence, and we're

00:51:05.550 --> 00:51:08.275 align:middle line:84%
going to try to figure
out theories about why

00:51:08.275 --> 00:51:09.525 align:middle line:90%
those are the special strings.

00:51:09.525 --> 00:51:15.800 align:middle line:90%


00:51:15.800 --> 00:51:19.310 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going
to do is we'll say just

00:51:19.310 --> 00:51:23.840 align:middle line:84%
as with "unlockable" we
were taking pairs of things

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:27.560 align:middle line:84%
and putting them together
to form larger things,

00:51:27.560 --> 00:51:28.730 align:middle line:90%
larger units.

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:32.120 align:middle line:84%
We'll do the same thing
here, only with words.

00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:35.120 align:middle line:84%
We had this operation,
we were calling it merge,

00:51:35.120 --> 00:51:37.250 align:middle line:84%
that takes pairs of things
and puts them together

00:51:37.250 --> 00:51:38.510 align:middle line:90%
into a larger thing.

00:51:38.510 --> 00:51:40.400 align:middle line:84%
And similarly here,
what we're going

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:43.730 align:middle line:84%
to do to create a sentence like
"I will find the red book,"

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:45.590 align:middle line:84%
we'll start with
just the end of it,

00:51:45.590 --> 00:51:47.780 align:middle line:84%
"find the red book"
is we're going

00:51:47.780 --> 00:51:49.820 align:middle line:84%
to take pairs of things
like "red" and "book"

00:51:49.820 --> 00:51:51.020 align:middle line:90%
and put them together.

00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:53.237 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll take
that unit that we've

00:51:53.237 --> 00:51:55.070 align:middle line:84%
created by putting
together "red" and "book"

00:51:55.070 --> 00:51:58.160 align:middle line:84%
and we'll put that together
with this word, "the."

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:02.420 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll take
that thing that we've

00:52:02.420 --> 00:52:07.850 align:middle line:84%
created by putting together "the
red book" and we'll add "find."

00:52:07.850 --> 00:52:10.820 align:middle line:84%
So just as with "unlockable,"
we were taking pairs of things

00:52:10.820 --> 00:52:14.435 align:middle line:84%
and putting them
together in pairs

00:52:14.435 --> 00:52:17.000 align:middle line:84%
to create these larger
and larger structures.

00:52:17.000 --> 00:52:20.565 align:middle line:84%
We're going to do the same
thing to create sentences out

00:52:20.565 --> 00:52:21.065 align:middle line:90%
of words.

00:52:21.065 --> 00:52:24.560 align:middle line:90%


00:52:24.560 --> 00:52:27.200 align:middle line:84%
This way of talking
about it has the virtue

00:52:27.200 --> 00:52:28.910 align:middle line:84%
of giving us a
vocabulary for talking

00:52:28.910 --> 00:52:30.910 align:middle line:84%
about those kinds of
observations we were making

00:52:30.910 --> 00:52:32.880 align:middle line:90%
on the last two slides.

00:52:32.880 --> 00:52:38.042 align:middle line:84%
So when we say "the red book" is
a unit that various things get

00:52:38.042 --> 00:52:40.250 align:middle line:84%
to apply to, things like
what I called topicalization

00:52:40.250 --> 00:52:42.890 align:middle line:84%
where you take a
chunk of the sentence

00:52:42.890 --> 00:52:46.822 align:middle line:84%
and put it at the beginning, and
it has some kind of emphasis.

00:52:46.822 --> 00:52:48.530 align:middle line:84%
Because this is syntax
and not semantics,

00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:52.280 align:middle line:84%
we won't worry too much
about what it means.

00:52:52.280 --> 00:52:54.170 align:middle line:84%
When we say that's
something you can

00:52:54.170 --> 00:52:56.960 align:middle line:84%
do to the string "the red
book," but not, for example,

00:52:56.960 --> 00:52:58.940 align:middle line:84%
to "find the red,"
this tree gives us

00:52:58.940 --> 00:53:00.140 align:middle line:90%
a way of talking about that.

00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:03.830 align:middle line:84%
There is a unit that we created
in the course of putting things

00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:06.980 align:middle line:84%
together in pairs that
is just "the red book."

00:53:06.980 --> 00:53:09.800 align:middle line:84%
it's the unit that
I've circled there.

00:53:09.800 --> 00:53:12.560 align:middle line:84%
But there is no unit
that we've created

00:53:12.560 --> 00:53:15.050 align:middle line:84%
as we've been putting these
pairs together that consists

00:53:15.050 --> 00:53:18.430 align:middle line:90%
just to "find the red."

00:53:18.430 --> 00:53:20.540 align:middle line:90%
Do people see that in this tree?

00:53:20.540 --> 00:53:23.590 align:middle line:84%
So there's a node in
the tree, if you want.

00:53:23.590 --> 00:53:28.330 align:middle line:84%
It's the one that I circled
in red that consists just

00:53:28.330 --> 00:53:30.760 align:middle line:90%
of the words "the red book."

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:32.830 align:middle line:84%
But there is no
thing that I could

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:35.815 align:middle line:84%
circle that would consist just
of the words, "find the red."

00:53:35.815 --> 00:53:38.137 align:middle line:90%


00:53:38.137 --> 00:53:39.720 align:middle line:84%
There are other
things I could circle.

00:53:39.720 --> 00:53:41.580 align:middle line:84%
I could have circled
the largest thing,

00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:44.280 align:middle line:84%
but that corresponds
to "find the red book."

00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:47.820 align:middle line:84%
Or I could have circled a
thing that consists just

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:48.780 align:middle line:90%
of "red" and "book."

00:53:48.780 --> 00:53:50.160 align:middle line:90%
It would just be "red book."

00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:52.800 align:middle line:84%
But there's nothing that's
just "find the red."

00:53:52.800 --> 00:53:54.750 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
we're going to relate

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:57.000 align:middle line:84%
to all those observations
we made on those two slides.

00:53:57.000 --> 00:54:05.340 align:middle line:90%


00:54:05.340 --> 00:54:08.027 align:middle line:84%
I've said this now
a couple of times,

00:54:08.027 --> 00:54:10.110 align:middle line:84%
this is meant to remind
you of stuff we were doing

00:54:10.110 --> 00:54:11.190 align:middle line:90%
when we were doing morphology.

00:54:11.190 --> 00:54:12.565 align:middle line:84%
When we were doing
morphology, we

00:54:12.565 --> 00:54:15.090 align:middle line:84%
were using this operation,
we called it merge,

00:54:15.090 --> 00:54:19.650 align:middle line:84%
that assembled pairs of
things and created new things.

00:54:19.650 --> 00:54:21.630 align:middle line:84%
When we were doing
morphology, we

00:54:21.630 --> 00:54:24.100 align:middle line:84%
were then saying when you put
these two things together,

00:54:24.100 --> 00:54:26.910 align:middle line:84%
you have a new thing, and
we're giving that thing

00:54:26.910 --> 00:54:31.140 align:middle line:84%
a label whose properties
are determined by the things

00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:32.950 align:middle line:90%
that you put together.

00:54:32.950 --> 00:54:37.128 align:middle line:84%
So when you put together
"un-" number one, and "lock,"

00:54:37.128 --> 00:54:38.670 align:middle line:84%
it's part of the
specification of un-

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:40.670 align:middle line:84%
number one that when you
combine it with a verb,

00:54:40.670 --> 00:54:42.510 align:middle line:90%
what you get is a verb.

00:54:42.510 --> 00:54:47.190 align:middle line:84%
So the tree on the left there,
"lock" is labeled as a verb,

00:54:47.190 --> 00:54:49.650 align:middle line:84%
and "unlock" is also
labeled as a verb.

00:54:49.650 --> 00:54:51.780 align:middle line:90%
People see that.

00:54:51.780 --> 00:54:54.910 align:middle line:84%
And then when you combine that
verb "unlock" with a "-able,"

00:54:54.910 --> 00:54:58.560 align:middle line:84%
it's a property of "-able"
that it merges with verbs

00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:01.507 align:middle line:84%
and the thing that you create
as a result is an adjective.

00:55:01.507 --> 00:55:03.090 align:middle line:84%
And so the whole
thing is "unlockable.

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:05.958 align:middle line:84%
This is what we were
doing with labels before.

00:55:05.958 --> 00:55:08.250 align:middle line:84%
We're going to want to do
something similar for syntax.

00:55:08.250 --> 00:55:10.500 align:middle line:84%
We're going to want to label
the parts of these trees.

00:55:10.500 --> 00:55:13.312 align:middle line:90%


00:55:13.312 --> 00:55:15.020 align:middle line:84%
What kinds of labels
are we going to use?

00:55:15.020 --> 00:55:18.352 align:middle line:84%
Well, look, I just gave
you all these diagnostics

00:55:18.352 --> 00:55:20.560 align:middle line:84%
to try to convince you that
when you say things like,

00:55:20.560 --> 00:55:23.080 align:middle line:84%
"I will find the red
book," "the red book"

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:29.050 align:middle line:84%
should be a unit, right,
phenomena like topicalization

00:55:29.050 --> 00:55:32.188 align:middle line:84%
should get to make
reference to it.

00:55:32.188 --> 00:55:33.730 align:middle line:84%
We have to decide
what to label that.

00:55:33.730 --> 00:55:37.570 align:middle line:84%
Notice there are a
bunch of things that

00:55:37.570 --> 00:55:38.960 align:middle line:90%
have all of those properties.

00:55:38.960 --> 00:55:42.310 align:middle line:84%
So if I said, "I will
find red books," well,

00:55:42.310 --> 00:55:44.620 align:middle line:84%
you'd be able to
topicalized "red books."

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:46.480 align:middle line:90%
So "I will find red books."

00:55:46.480 --> 00:55:48.130 align:middle line:90%
Or "red books I will find."

00:55:48.130 --> 00:55:52.060 align:middle line:84%
"I will find red books," and
you're amazed: "Red books?"

00:55:52.060 --> 00:55:54.655 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, "I will find those
red books about linguistics."

00:55:54.655 --> 00:55:56.530 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, "those red books
about linguistics,"

00:55:56.530 --> 00:56:00.610 align:middle line:90%
that's a unit a similar kind.

00:56:00.610 --> 00:56:03.280 align:middle line:84%
You can say "Those red
books about linguistics,

00:56:03.280 --> 00:56:03.940 align:middle line:90%
I will find."

00:56:03.940 --> 00:56:07.570 align:middle line:84%
Yes, so that's a unit we want
to be able to make reference to.

00:56:07.570 --> 00:56:10.080 align:middle line:84%
"I will find books"
similarly "books I will find"

00:56:10.080 --> 00:56:11.230 align:middle line:90%
or "I will find books."

00:56:11.230 --> 00:56:11.830 align:middle line:90%
"Books?"

00:56:11.830 --> 00:56:12.850 align:middle line:90%
You're amazed.

00:56:12.850 --> 00:56:15.100 align:middle line:84%
So "books" by
itself is apparently

00:56:15.100 --> 00:56:17.350 align:middle line:90%
a unit of the same kind.

00:56:17.350 --> 00:56:22.345 align:middle line:84%
So apparently the
important part of that,

00:56:22.345 --> 00:56:24.520 align:middle line:84%
the thing that all of
those have in common,

00:56:24.520 --> 00:56:27.290 align:middle line:90%
is that they contain a noun.

00:56:27.290 --> 00:56:31.710 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to name
that phrase after the fact

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:33.490 align:middle line:90%
that it contains a noun.

00:56:33.490 --> 00:56:37.410 align:middle line:84%
So when we put together
"red" and "book," what we get

00:56:37.410 --> 00:56:40.350 align:middle line:84%
has properties that are
determined by the fact

00:56:40.350 --> 00:56:42.550 align:middle line:90%
that it contains a noun.

00:56:42.550 --> 00:56:45.030 align:middle line:84%
If there were no noun, it
wouldn't have those properties.

00:56:45.030 --> 00:56:46.650 align:middle line:90%
Similarly, with "the red book."

00:56:46.650 --> 00:56:49.230 align:middle line:90%


00:56:49.230 --> 00:56:52.470 align:middle line:84%
The things that can go in
that slot, the things that

00:56:52.470 --> 00:56:56.010 align:middle line:84%
can go after "find," we just
saw in the last slide, there

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:58.810 align:middle line:84%
are various kinds of things
that can be larger or smaller.

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:00.610 align:middle line:90%
What they all contain is a noun.

00:57:00.610 --> 00:57:02.430 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
name that thing

00:57:02.430 --> 00:57:05.430 align:middle line:84%
after those kinds of units,
after the fact that they

00:57:05.430 --> 00:57:06.090 align:middle line:90%
contain a noun.

00:57:06.090 --> 00:57:07.590 align:middle line:90%
We'll give it the label "noun."

00:57:07.590 --> 00:57:10.370 align:middle line:90%


00:57:10.370 --> 00:57:13.167 align:middle line:84%
Kind of like when we added "un-"
to "lock" and got "unlock,"

00:57:13.167 --> 00:57:14.750 align:middle line:84%
we said, oh yeah,
this is also a verb.

00:57:14.750 --> 00:57:17.720 align:middle line:84%
It acts like a verb
in every other way.

00:57:17.720 --> 00:57:19.970 align:middle line:84%
You can use it as a verb:
"I unlock the door."

00:57:19.970 --> 00:57:23.060 align:middle line:84%
You can add things to it
that can be added to verbs,

00:57:23.060 --> 00:57:24.170 align:middle line:90%
like "-able."

00:57:24.170 --> 00:57:26.060 align:middle line:84%
So similarly here,
when we're trying

00:57:26.060 --> 00:57:29.032 align:middle line:84%
to decide what kinds of
things can go in this slot,

00:57:29.032 --> 00:57:31.490 align:middle line:84%
the slot that's right after
"find," the thing that they all

00:57:31.490 --> 00:57:33.610 align:middle line:84%
have in common is that
they contain nouns.

00:57:33.610 --> 00:57:35.360 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to name
them after the fact

00:57:35.360 --> 00:57:36.360 align:middle line:90%
that they contain nouns.

00:57:36.360 --> 00:57:38.720 align:middle line:84%
We'll call them all
"extended nouns."

00:57:38.720 --> 00:57:39.220 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:57:39.220 --> 00:57:42.840 align:middle line:90%


00:57:42.840 --> 00:57:48.600 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, there is another unit
in "I will find the red book,"

00:57:48.600 --> 00:57:52.620 align:middle line:84%
not just "the red book," which
we said is this extended noun.

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:57.340 align:middle line:84%
There's also another
phrase, "find the red book,"

00:57:57.340 --> 00:57:58.560 align:middle line:90%
which is also a unit.

00:57:58.560 --> 00:58:01.978 align:middle line:84%
And it was the unit that we
were constructing in the slides

00:58:01.978 --> 00:58:04.020 align:middle line:84%
when I was showing you
trees for how to construct

00:58:04.020 --> 00:58:05.130 align:middle line:90%
"find the red book."

00:58:05.130 --> 00:58:10.680 align:middle line:84%
and it passes these tests
for unithood, constituency.

00:58:10.680 --> 00:58:15.630 align:middle line:84%
You can say, "Find
the red book I will,"

00:58:15.630 --> 00:58:20.130 align:middle line:84%
not only if you are Yoda, but
also if you say something like,

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:21.900 align:middle line:84%
"I said that I would
find the red book,

00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:23.850 align:middle line:90%
and find the red book I will."

00:58:23.850 --> 00:58:27.840 align:middle line:84%
It's more or less OK
sentences of English.

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:31.810 align:middle line:84%
Or if I say "I will find the
red book" and you are amazed,

00:58:31.810 --> 00:58:33.510 align:middle line:84%
you can say, "Find
the red book?"

00:58:33.510 --> 00:58:35.340 align:middle line:84%
(No one has ever
found the red book.

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:40.440 align:middle line:84%
It's been lost for centuries.)
And various other things that

00:58:40.440 --> 00:58:42.150 align:middle line:90%
are on this slide.

00:58:42.150 --> 00:58:44.778 align:middle line:84%
"Find the red book" is also
a constituent, also a unit

00:58:44.778 --> 00:58:46.320 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to
want syntax to be

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:48.330 align:middle line:90%
able to make reference to.

00:58:48.330 --> 00:58:53.280 align:middle line:84%
And just as we said "the red
book," the important part

00:58:53.280 --> 00:58:55.500 align:middle line:84%
of "the red book"
is the noun, that's

00:58:55.500 --> 00:58:57.990 align:middle line:84%
the thing that determines
that that phrase is

00:58:57.990 --> 00:58:59.397 align:middle line:90%
OK in that place.

00:58:59.397 --> 00:59:01.980 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there are various phrases
that you can put in that place

00:59:01.980 --> 00:59:03.810 align:middle line:90%
and they all have nouns in them.

00:59:03.810 --> 00:59:06.510 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, all the things
you can put in this place

00:59:06.510 --> 00:59:08.407 align:middle line:90%
have verbs in them.

00:59:08.407 --> 00:59:09.990 align:middle line:84%
They don't have to
have anything else.

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:12.240 align:middle line:84%
You can say things
like "I will leave,"

00:59:12.240 --> 00:59:15.540 align:middle line:84%
and then "leave" has all the
properties we just ran through.

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:18.120 align:middle line:84%
"I said I would leave,
and leave I will."

00:59:18.120 --> 00:59:21.240 align:middle line:84%
"Leave" is a unit of the same
kind as "find the red book."

00:59:21.240 --> 00:59:24.120 align:middle line:84%
Or "I will leave"
and you're amazed.

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:26.010 align:middle line:90%
You can say, "Leave?"

00:59:26.010 --> 00:59:28.872 align:middle line:84%
So "leave" is a unit of the same
kind as "find the red book."

00:59:28.872 --> 00:59:30.330 align:middle line:84%
So the important
part for this part

00:59:30.330 --> 00:59:34.410 align:middle line:84%
is the verb, the
part that determines

00:59:34.410 --> 00:59:37.180 align:middle line:84%
that that's the kind of phrase
that can go in that position.

00:59:37.180 --> 00:59:39.390 align:middle line:84%
And so when we're
putting together

00:59:39.390 --> 00:59:42.660 align:middle line:84%
"find" with "the red book"
we said "the red book"

00:59:42.660 --> 00:59:44.340 align:middle line:90%
is kind of an extended noun.

00:59:44.340 --> 00:59:46.770 align:middle line:84%
It's a large unit
whose special property

00:59:46.770 --> 00:59:48.750 align:middle line:90%
is that it contains a noun.

00:59:48.750 --> 00:59:52.530 align:middle line:84%
"Find the red book" we're going
to give that the label verb,

00:59:52.530 --> 00:59:55.870 align:middle line:84%
because having a verb is
the important part for that.

00:59:55.870 --> 00:59:58.110 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, so far so good?

00:59:58.110 --> 01:00:01.290 align:middle line:90%


01:00:01.290 --> 01:00:06.000 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, one more.

01:00:06.000 --> 01:00:08.430 align:middle line:84%
You can say "I will find
the book in the garage."

01:00:08.430 --> 01:00:12.330 align:middle line:84%
"In the garage" is a
unit, it's a constituent.

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:14.250 align:middle line:84%
It's a phrase, it's the
kind of thing syntax

01:00:14.250 --> 01:00:16.930 align:middle line:90%
gets to care about.

01:00:16.930 --> 01:00:19.545 align:middle line:84%
And again, if I say, "I will
find the book in the garage"

01:00:19.545 --> 01:00:23.520 align:middle line:84%
and you're amazed, you
can say "In the garage?"

01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:25.680 align:middle line:84%
If I want to, I can
topicalize "in the garage."

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:29.010 align:middle line:84%
I can say things like, "in the
garage I will find the book."

01:00:29.010 --> 01:00:32.490 align:middle line:90%


01:00:32.490 --> 01:00:33.990 align:middle line:90%
It's a prepositional phrase.

01:00:33.990 --> 01:00:39.510 align:middle line:84%
The kinds of things that can
go in a prepositional phrase

01:00:39.510 --> 01:00:42.930 align:middle line:84%
include prepositions
and noun phrases.

01:00:42.930 --> 01:00:44.880 align:middle line:84%
"The garage" is
another noun phrase.

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:46.620 align:middle line:84%
There are also
prepositional phrases

01:00:46.620 --> 01:00:48.540 align:middle line:84%
that seem to just
contain a preposition,

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:53.460 align:middle line:84%
like, "I will look up,"
where, again, if I say "I

01:00:53.460 --> 01:00:56.100 align:middle line:84%
will look up" and you're
amazed, you can say, "Up?"

01:00:56.100 --> 01:01:01.500 align:middle line:84%
(Why will you look up?) "I
said I would look up, and up

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:02.130 align:middle line:90%
I will look"--

01:01:02.130 --> 01:01:04.960 align:middle line:90%


01:01:04.960 --> 01:01:07.480 align:middle line:90%
Maybe.

01:01:07.480 --> 01:01:08.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:08.230 --> 01:01:09.647 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do
we know that "up"

01:01:09.647 --> 01:01:12.983 align:middle line:84%
isn't like an adverb
describing "look"?

01:01:12.983 --> 01:01:13.900 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:01:13.900 --> 01:01:18.230 align:middle line:90%


01:01:18.230 --> 01:01:20.340 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:23.140 align:middle line:90%
Yep, yep, yep.

01:01:23.140 --> 01:01:24.790 align:middle line:90%
Yes sir?

01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:25.450 align:middle line:90%
Adverb.

01:01:25.450 --> 01:01:29.350 align:middle line:84%
Adverb is a funny
word because there

01:01:29.350 --> 01:01:34.640 align:middle line:84%
are a lot of things
that can be used.

01:01:34.640 --> 01:01:39.950 align:middle line:84%
So if by adverb, we mean
thing that modifies the verb,

01:01:39.950 --> 01:01:44.150 align:middle line:84%
there are a lot of things that
can be used that way, including

01:01:44.150 --> 01:01:48.440 align:middle line:84%
adverbs like "I will leave
quickly," where "quickly"

01:01:48.440 --> 01:01:50.270 align:middle line:90%
is an adverb.

01:01:50.270 --> 01:01:53.450 align:middle line:84%
But also prepositional
phrases like

01:01:53.450 --> 01:01:57.920 align:middle line:84%
"I will leave in a chariot"
where the prepositional phrase

01:01:57.920 --> 01:02:01.550 align:middle line:90%
is like an adverb.

01:02:01.550 --> 01:02:06.800 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe even noun
phrases like "I

01:02:06.800 --> 01:02:09.170 align:middle line:84%
will leave the day
after tomorrow,"

01:02:09.170 --> 01:02:13.040 align:middle line:84%
where "the day after tomorrow"
sure looks like a noun phrase.

01:02:13.040 --> 01:02:17.030 align:middle line:84%
It's got a noun in it, "day,"
and then "the" before that,

01:02:17.030 --> 01:02:20.790 align:middle line:84%
but we know that can go at the
beginnings of noun phrases.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:24.350 align:middle line:84%
So there are probably--
so the word "adverb"

01:02:24.350 --> 01:02:27.128 align:middle line:84%
can be used to cover
a bunch of things,

01:02:27.128 --> 01:02:29.420 align:middle line:84%
including things that we
don't have any other word for,

01:02:29.420 --> 01:02:33.080 align:middle line:84%
like "quickly," which is
an adverb, but also things

01:02:33.080 --> 01:02:34.790 align:middle line:84%
that we would give
other labels to

01:02:34.790 --> 01:02:37.410 align:middle line:84%
like "noun phrase" or
"prepositional phrase."

01:02:37.410 --> 01:02:39.260 align:middle line:84%
And so I think you
might be right that this

01:02:39.260 --> 01:02:42.680 align:middle line:84%
is an adverb in the sense
that it modifies the verb.

01:02:42.680 --> 01:02:44.660 align:middle line:84%
I think I might also
be right in the sense

01:02:44.660 --> 01:02:46.910 align:middle line:84%
that it's a prepositional
phrase that's

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:48.770 align:middle line:90%
being used as adverbially.

01:02:48.770 --> 01:02:49.680 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:51.800 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm trying to do
is work out a way for you

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:56.240 align:middle line:84%
and I to both be right, which is
always my goal in discussions.

01:02:56.240 --> 01:02:57.400 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:02:57.400 --> 01:02:59.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What about a
sentence like "I will wake up"?

01:02:59.980 --> 01:03:01.600 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
"I will wake up."

01:03:01.600 --> 01:03:04.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does it make
sense to say [INAUDIBLE]??

01:03:04.880 --> 01:03:06.760 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
that's a nice example.

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:07.600 align:middle line:90%
"I will wake up."

01:03:07.600 --> 01:03:10.630 align:middle line:84%
So probably-- I mean, I just
tried to convince everybody

01:03:10.630 --> 01:03:12.010 align:middle line:90%
that it was OK to say up--

01:03:12.010 --> 01:03:14.440 align:middle line:84%
I said, "I would look
up and up I will look."

01:03:14.440 --> 01:03:17.880 align:middle line:84%
But "I said I would wake
up and up I will wake,"

01:03:17.880 --> 01:03:20.920 align:middle line:90%
I'm not even going to try.

01:03:20.920 --> 01:03:25.060 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, I think,
if I say "I will wake up"

01:03:25.060 --> 01:03:28.180 align:middle line:84%
and you're amazed, you're
not going to say, "Up?"

01:03:28.180 --> 01:03:32.300 align:middle line:90%


01:03:32.300 --> 01:03:33.123 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:33.123 --> 01:03:34.040 align:middle line:90%
That's a nice example.

01:03:34.040 --> 01:03:39.980 align:middle line:90%


01:03:39.980 --> 01:03:44.120 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of things
that look like prepositions that

01:03:44.120 --> 01:03:47.750 align:middle line:84%
combine in an interesting
way with verbs in English

01:03:47.750 --> 01:03:49.610 align:middle line:84%
and a lot of other
Germanic languages.

01:03:49.610 --> 01:03:51.290 align:middle line:90%
And "wake up" is one of those.

01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:55.830 align:middle line:90%


01:03:55.830 --> 01:03:58.310 align:middle line:84%
And this is actually
the kind of example

01:03:58.310 --> 01:04:02.000 align:middle line:84%
that I was exploiting in the
first slides I was showing you

01:04:02.000 --> 01:04:07.610 align:middle line:84%
about syntax, even the
ones on the first day

01:04:07.610 --> 01:04:09.350 align:middle line:90%
that you can say--

01:04:09.350 --> 01:04:12.410 align:middle line:84%
so wake up, you can
say "I will wake up."

01:04:12.410 --> 01:04:16.790 align:middle line:90%


01:04:16.790 --> 01:04:23.885 align:middle line:84%
You can also say, "I
will wake up the cats."

01:04:23.885 --> 01:04:28.070 align:middle line:84%
It's unwise but
it's grammatical.

01:04:28.070 --> 01:04:33.140 align:middle line:84%
But I think "up the cats" is
not a prepositional phrase.

01:04:33.140 --> 01:04:37.198 align:middle line:84%
Notice, for example, that if
I say I will wake up the cats

01:04:37.198 --> 01:04:38.990 align:middle line:84%
and you're astonished
as you might well be,

01:04:38.990 --> 01:04:41.510 align:middle line:90%
you can't say "Up the cats?"

01:04:41.510 --> 01:04:45.290 align:middle line:84%
And "I said I would wake
up the cats and up the cats

01:04:45.290 --> 01:04:47.660 align:middle line:90%
I will wake," this is no good.

01:04:47.660 --> 01:04:48.440 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:04:48.440 --> 01:04:51.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think that's
not because kind of combined

01:04:51.080 --> 01:04:53.090 align:middle line:84%
with the verb you can
say "I will wake up"

01:04:53.090 --> 01:04:54.850 align:middle line:84%
but you can't say,
"I will wake down."

01:04:54.850 --> 01:04:55.360 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:04:55.360 --> 01:04:56.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:04:56.150 --> 01:04:56.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:04:56.650 --> 01:04:59.560 align:middle line:84%
So I think-- we don't want
to think of "up the cats"

01:04:59.560 --> 01:05:00.130 align:middle line:90%
as a unit.

01:05:00.130 --> 01:05:02.980 align:middle line:84%
We want to think of
"wake up" as a unit

01:05:02.980 --> 01:05:05.408 align:middle line:90%
that has the cats as an object.

01:05:05.408 --> 01:05:06.950 align:middle line:84%
So this is the point
you were making,

01:05:06.950 --> 01:05:09.450 align:middle line:84%
and I think that you are also
making, that there's something

01:05:09.450 --> 01:05:11.240 align:middle line:90%
special about that interaction.

01:05:11.240 --> 01:05:11.980 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:11.980 --> 01:05:14.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is that an interesting
pair, "Are you up for lunch?"

01:05:14.830 --> 01:05:16.150 align:middle line:90%
or "Are you down for lunch?"

01:05:16.150 --> 01:05:17.424 align:middle line:90%
They mean the same thing.

01:05:17.424 --> 01:05:19.722 align:middle line:90%


01:05:19.722 --> 01:05:21.430 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: And
so we are learning--

01:05:21.430 --> 01:05:23.770 align:middle line:84%
this is why I'm glad I'm
not a physicist, yes.

01:05:23.770 --> 01:05:26.710 align:middle line:84%
So I work in the domain
in which up and down can

01:05:26.710 --> 01:05:27.620 align:middle line:90%
be the same thing.

01:05:27.620 --> 01:05:28.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:28.960 --> 01:05:31.720 align:middle line:84%
If I were a physicist, then
if NASA were to hire me,

01:05:31.720 --> 01:05:35.560 align:middle line:84%
the spacecraft would have
all kinds of problems.

01:05:35.560 --> 01:05:37.150 align:middle line:90%
That's a nice example.

01:05:37.150 --> 01:05:40.540 align:middle line:84%
Relatedly, "I will
wake up the cat" is OK.

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:46.180 align:middle line:84%
You can also say, "I
will wake the cats up,"

01:05:46.180 --> 01:05:53.485 align:middle line:84%
which is different from "I
will walk up the stairs."

01:05:53.485 --> 01:05:56.560 align:middle line:90%


01:05:56.560 --> 01:06:00.460 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say, "I will walk
the stairs up," I think.

01:06:00.460 --> 01:06:04.700 align:middle line:84%
And relatedly-- I exploited
this in an earlier slide--

01:06:04.700 --> 01:06:09.130 align:middle line:84%
you can say, "I
will wake him up."

01:06:09.130 --> 01:06:10.330 align:middle line:90%
"I will wake them up."

01:06:10.330 --> 01:06:12.910 align:middle line:90%


01:06:12.910 --> 01:06:19.900 align:middle line:84%
But "I will walk
them up," no good.

01:06:19.900 --> 01:06:21.680 align:middle line:84%
In fact, it's the
other way around.

01:06:21.680 --> 01:06:22.390 align:middle line:90%
I think, right?

01:06:22.390 --> 01:06:23.448 align:middle line:90%
"I will walk them up."

01:06:23.448 --> 01:06:25.240 align:middle line:84%
What are you going to
do with those stairs?

01:06:25.240 --> 01:06:26.980 align:middle line:90%
"I'm going to walk them up."

01:06:26.980 --> 01:06:28.300 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:06:28.300 --> 01:06:30.700 align:middle line:84%
You have to say I
will walk up them.

01:06:30.700 --> 01:06:33.190 align:middle line:84%
You cannot say, "I
will wake up them."

01:06:33.190 --> 01:06:35.440 align:middle line:84%
You have to say, "I
will wake them up."

01:06:35.440 --> 01:06:38.170 align:middle line:90%
So these "up"s are different.

01:06:38.170 --> 01:06:41.380 align:middle line:84%
So "up the stairs" is
a prepositional phrase.

01:06:41.380 --> 01:06:43.480 align:middle line:84%
"Up the cats" is not a
prepositional phrase.

01:06:43.480 --> 01:06:46.180 align:middle line:84%
We need different structures
for these verb phrases,

01:06:46.180 --> 01:06:48.040 align:middle line:90%
and we will develop them.

01:06:48.040 --> 01:06:48.870 align:middle line:90%
You had a--

01:06:48.870 --> 01:06:49.495 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:06:49.495 --> 01:06:51.430 align:middle line:84%
I was also going to
say, but you can say--

01:06:51.430 --> 01:06:53.222 align:middle line:84%
if you're taking someone
home, you can say,

01:06:53.222 --> 01:06:55.870 align:middle line:84%
"I will walk you
up to your room."

01:06:55.870 --> 01:06:57.340 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:06:57.340 --> 01:06:59.110 align:middle line:84%
"I will walk you
up to your room."

01:06:59.110 --> 01:07:04.570 align:middle line:84%
You can say, "I will walk
the student up to her room."

01:07:04.570 --> 01:07:08.890 align:middle line:84%
You can't say, "I will walk
up the student to her room."

01:07:08.890 --> 01:07:10.393 align:middle line:90%
Can you?

01:07:10.393 --> 01:07:12.310 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But yeah, so
it's like the opposite.

01:07:12.310 --> 01:07:13.227 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Wait.

01:07:13.227 --> 01:07:15.910 align:middle line:84%
Can you say, "I will walk up
to the student to her room?"

01:07:15.910 --> 01:07:16.577 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No.

01:07:16.577 --> 01:07:17.410 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No.

01:07:17.410 --> 01:07:19.930 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That implies you're
walking on the student, which--

01:07:19.930 --> 01:07:20.410 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, OK.

01:07:20.410 --> 01:07:22.000 align:middle line:84%
So if the student
is lying down, yeah,

01:07:22.000 --> 01:07:25.060 align:middle line:84%
and I'm using her as
a ladder then, yes.

01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:29.620 align:middle line:84%
But if not-- so I think "I
will walk the student up

01:07:29.620 --> 01:07:32.440 align:middle line:84%
to her room" is different
from all of these,

01:07:32.440 --> 01:07:34.030 align:middle line:90%
actually, kind of interestingly.

01:07:34.030 --> 01:07:38.680 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe "up" is
modifying "to her room."

01:07:38.680 --> 01:07:40.960 align:middle line:84%
We want there to be a
constituent "up to her room."

01:07:40.960 --> 01:07:44.800 align:middle line:84%
Notice that if I say, "I
will walk her up to her room"

01:07:44.800 --> 01:07:47.800 align:middle line:84%
and you're amazed, you
can say, "Up to her room?"

01:07:47.800 --> 01:07:49.960 align:middle line:84%
which suggests that
that's a constituent.

01:07:49.960 --> 01:07:53.440 align:middle line:90%


01:07:53.440 --> 01:07:56.800 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's a nice third
thing I should talk about when

01:07:56.800 --> 01:07:57.760 align:middle line:90%
I'm talking about this.

01:07:57.760 --> 01:08:00.620 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

01:08:00.620 --> 01:08:01.885 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yeah?

01:08:01.885 --> 01:08:03.280 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
I had a question.

01:08:03.280 --> 01:08:06.140 align:middle line:84%
So I think there's something
different between waking

01:08:06.140 --> 01:08:08.240 align:middle line:90%
up or walking up.

01:08:08.240 --> 01:08:12.130 align:middle line:84%
In a sense, you could
say, [INAUDIBLE] and that

01:08:12.130 --> 01:08:13.510 align:middle line:90%
would make sense.

01:08:13.510 --> 01:08:16.500 align:middle line:84%
So it's kind of
like, I don't know,

01:08:16.500 --> 01:08:20.010 align:middle line:84%
an adjective, in some sense,
is describing [? state. ?]

01:08:20.010 --> 01:08:22.450 align:middle line:84%
But at the same time,
it's not an adjective.

01:08:22.450 --> 01:08:24.479 align:middle line:90%
It's describing a verb.

01:08:24.479 --> 01:08:28.080 align:middle line:84%
So I was wondering if
there's any big distinction

01:08:28.080 --> 01:08:32.170 align:middle line:84%
between waking up and like
walking up [INAUDIBLE]..

01:08:32.170 --> 01:08:34.550 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So that's
a very nice example.

01:08:34.550 --> 01:08:38.560 align:middle line:84%
So in a way, waking
the cats up, it's

01:08:38.560 --> 01:08:41.380 align:middle line:84%
a little bit like
painting the cats red.

01:08:41.380 --> 01:08:45.310 align:middle line:84%
Not in very many
ways, but in this way.

01:08:45.310 --> 01:08:48.069 align:middle line:84%
If you paint the cats
red, you paint the cats.

01:08:48.069 --> 01:08:51.640 align:middle line:84%
And as a result of
that, the cats are red.

01:08:51.640 --> 01:08:54.354 align:middle line:84%
The cats change into cats that
were not red before and now

01:08:54.354 --> 01:08:55.479 align:middle line:90%
they are cats that are red.

01:08:55.479 --> 01:08:58.000 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, if you
wake the cats up,

01:08:58.000 --> 01:08:59.710 align:middle line:90%
the cats were not up before.

01:08:59.710 --> 01:09:01.660 align:middle line:84%
But when you're done,
the cats are up.

01:09:01.660 --> 01:09:02.740 align:middle line:90%
And possibly also red.

01:09:02.740 --> 01:09:06.790 align:middle line:84%
It depends on how
you did it, I guess.

01:09:06.790 --> 01:09:09.130 align:middle line:84%
So I think you're absolutely
right that we want "wake

01:09:09.130 --> 01:09:11.887 align:middle line:90%
the cats up" to have "up."

01:09:11.887 --> 01:09:12.970 align:middle line:90%
"I will wake up the cats."

01:09:12.970 --> 01:09:15.609 align:middle line:84%
We want "up" to not be
a preposition that's

01:09:15.609 --> 01:09:18.109 align:middle line:84%
combining with the cats to
be a prepositional phrase.

01:09:18.109 --> 01:09:19.160 align:middle line:90%
It's something else.

01:09:19.160 --> 01:09:21.370 align:middle line:84%
It's like a predicate
of some kind.

01:09:21.370 --> 01:09:24.160 align:middle line:84%
It's like "red" in "I
will paint the cats red."

01:09:24.160 --> 01:09:28.330 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, as we study
these verb phrases further,

01:09:28.330 --> 01:09:31.720 align:middle line:84%
we'll want to have structures
that give them that character.

01:09:31.720 --> 01:09:33.670 align:middle line:90%
That was a very nice example.

01:09:33.670 --> 01:09:36.100 align:middle line:84%
Did you have a
question a while ago?

01:09:36.100 --> 01:09:36.600 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:37.710 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

01:09:37.710 --> 01:09:41.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think
that that does fit.

01:09:41.250 --> 01:09:42.637 align:middle line:90%
You can also wake the cats.

01:09:42.637 --> 01:09:44.470 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes,
you can wake the cats.

01:09:44.470 --> 01:09:45.580 align:middle line:90%
You can also paint the cats.

01:09:45.580 --> 01:09:46.600 align:middle line:90%
You shouldn't, but you can.

01:09:46.600 --> 01:09:47.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:09:47.100 --> 01:09:48.220 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:09:48.220 --> 01:09:49.447 align:middle line:90%
Did you have a question?

01:09:49.447 --> 01:09:50.072 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:09:50.072 --> 01:09:53.520 align:middle line:84%
So with the example of
walking her up to her room,

01:09:53.520 --> 01:09:58.055 align:middle line:84%
I think you need to say,
"I will walk her up."

01:09:58.055 --> 01:09:58.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes.

01:09:58.930 --> 01:09:59.530 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Right?

01:09:59.530 --> 01:10:00.405 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yep.

01:10:00.405 --> 01:10:02.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think because
the "up" is modifying

01:10:02.890 --> 01:10:05.440 align:middle line:84%
"to her room," but I
don't think [? so. ?]

01:10:05.440 --> 01:10:07.450 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: It
doesn't have to, does it?

01:10:07.450 --> 01:10:08.800 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:10:08.800 --> 01:10:10.120 align:middle line:90%
I will walk her up.

01:10:10.120 --> 01:10:12.370 align:middle line:90%
I will walk her up to her room.

01:10:12.370 --> 01:10:15.130 align:middle line:84%
I will-- but similarly,
you can walk the student--

01:10:15.130 --> 01:10:17.365 align:middle line:84%
so I keep going to
the student from her,

01:10:17.365 --> 01:10:19.240 align:middle line:84%
because there's a
difference between pronouns

01:10:19.240 --> 01:10:20.650 align:middle line:90%
and non-pronouns.

01:10:20.650 --> 01:10:23.560 align:middle line:84%
You can wake up the cats or
you can wake the cats up.

01:10:23.560 --> 01:10:26.350 align:middle line:84%
You can wake them up, and
you cannot wake up them.

01:10:26.350 --> 01:10:29.680 align:middle line:84%
So a pronoun has to go
before this kind of "up,"

01:10:29.680 --> 01:10:32.440 align:middle line:84%
but the cats can
go on either side.

01:10:32.440 --> 01:10:34.780 align:middle line:84%
So "I will walk her up,"
you're absolutely right.

01:10:34.780 --> 01:10:35.980 align:middle line:90%
That's the way to say that.

01:10:35.980 --> 01:10:39.400 align:middle line:84%
But I think you also have
to walk the student up.

01:10:39.400 --> 01:10:43.790 align:middle line:84%
You can't walk up the student,
unless the student is lying

01:10:43.790 --> 01:10:45.200 align:middle line:90%
down and you're walking on her.

01:10:45.200 --> 01:10:48.230 align:middle line:84%
But if you mean you're going
to walk with the student

01:10:48.230 --> 01:10:51.620 align:middle line:84%
so that the two of
you are up, so we

01:10:51.620 --> 01:10:54.530 align:middle line:84%
want "walk the student" up
to be different from "wake

01:10:54.530 --> 01:10:58.100 align:middle line:84%
the cats up," and maybe
also different from "walk

01:10:58.100 --> 01:10:59.550 align:middle line:90%
up the stairs."

01:10:59.550 --> 01:11:02.330 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's a third
kind of thing, which,

01:11:02.330 --> 01:11:03.952 align:middle line:84%
as we work further
on this, I'm--

01:11:03.952 --> 01:11:05.660 align:middle line:84%
in a way, I'm glad
that we're running out

01:11:05.660 --> 01:11:07.970 align:middle line:84%
of time because it
means that I have a week

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:09.650 align:middle line:90%
to create slides about this.

01:11:09.650 --> 01:11:13.950 align:middle line:84%
But we're going to want
different structures for this.

01:11:13.950 --> 01:11:15.950 align:middle line:84%
You guys are making
excellent points about this.

01:11:15.950 --> 01:11:17.750 align:middle line:84%
So we're developing
probes into the structure

01:11:17.750 --> 01:11:19.292 align:middle line:84%
of the inside of
the verb phrase that

01:11:19.292 --> 01:11:23.750 align:middle line:84%
mean that we're going to need
at least three different kinds

01:11:23.750 --> 01:11:33.740 align:middle line:84%
of structures for a sequence
that looks like verb, them, up,

01:11:33.740 --> 01:11:36.110 align:middle line:90%
or verb, up, them.

01:11:36.110 --> 01:11:38.630 align:middle line:90%


01:11:38.630 --> 01:11:41.630 align:middle line:84%
So we've got now three kinds
of examples to talk about.

01:11:41.630 --> 01:11:49.040 align:middle line:84%
There's "wake them up,"
there's "walk her up,"

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:55.620 align:middle line:84%
and there's "walk up them" as
in "I walked up the stairs."

01:11:55.620 --> 01:11:58.220 align:middle line:84%
And that seems to be three
different things that we need

01:11:58.220 --> 01:12:00.260 align:middle line:90%
three different structures for.

01:12:00.260 --> 01:12:05.630 align:middle line:84%
But for "walk up them" we want
"up" and "them" to combine

01:12:05.630 --> 01:12:07.040 align:middle line:90%
to be a propositional phrase.

01:12:07.040 --> 01:12:09.387 align:middle line:84%
But for these other two,
we want something else.

01:12:09.387 --> 01:12:11.720 align:middle line:84%
We're going to want to circle
around and try to find out

01:12:11.720 --> 01:12:12.950 align:middle line:90%
what that other thing is.

01:12:12.950 --> 01:12:13.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:12:13.640 --> 01:12:15.220 align:middle line:90%
Am I getting at your point?

01:12:15.220 --> 01:12:16.220 align:middle line:90%
You're absolutely right.

01:12:16.220 --> 01:12:20.810 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so we need a
way of covering that.

01:12:20.810 --> 01:12:21.970 align:middle line:90%
Lots of people had hands.

01:12:21.970 --> 01:12:23.660 align:middle line:90%
Does anybody else-- yeah?

01:12:23.660 --> 01:12:30.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I was going to suggest
something else [INAUDIBLE] Even

01:12:30.550 --> 01:12:35.310 align:middle line:90%
though you can't say, walk the--

01:12:35.310 --> 01:12:37.645 align:middle line:84%
"walk the stairs
up to your room."

01:12:37.645 --> 01:12:38.770 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "I walk--"

01:12:38.770 --> 01:12:41.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You could potentially
say, "Climb the ladder

01:12:41.410 --> 01:12:42.220 align:middle line:90%
to your room?"

01:12:42.220 --> 01:12:43.137 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:12:43.137 --> 01:12:45.550 align:middle line:84%
That's because you
can climb a ladder.

01:12:45.550 --> 01:12:49.870 align:middle line:84%
So you can climb a ladder, and
you can go up to your room.

01:12:49.870 --> 01:12:52.180 align:middle line:84%
That's the one where I
think we want "up" possibly

01:12:52.180 --> 01:12:53.590 align:middle line:90%
to modify to "your room."

01:12:53.590 --> 01:12:54.520 align:middle line:90%
Or maybe not.

01:12:54.520 --> 01:12:57.190 align:middle line:84%
Maybe this was your point, maybe
we want "up" and "to your room"

01:12:57.190 --> 01:12:59.740 align:middle line:90%
to be separate adverbs.

01:12:59.740 --> 01:13:01.600 align:middle line:84%
But just like you can
walk up to your room,

01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:03.910 align:middle line:84%
you can climb a ladder
up to your room.

01:13:03.910 --> 01:13:05.290 align:middle line:90%
The ladder isn't--

01:13:05.290 --> 01:13:05.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: --or the stairs.

01:13:05.680 --> 01:13:06.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:13:06.290 --> 01:13:07.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:07.000 --> 01:13:08.740 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, to
the extent that you

01:13:08.740 --> 01:13:11.690 align:middle line:84%
can say "He's
walking the stairs,"

01:13:11.690 --> 01:13:13.330 align:middle line:84%
which I think you
can sort of say,

01:13:13.330 --> 01:13:16.870 align:middle line:84%
it means he's on
the stairs, walking.

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:19.730 align:middle line:84%
I think that's what
you're doing there.

01:13:19.730 --> 01:13:22.420 align:middle line:84%
I hate to do it, but I want to
ruthlessly squelch everything

01:13:22.420 --> 01:13:23.620 align:middle line:84%
else you guys want
to say, because I

01:13:23.620 --> 01:13:25.630 align:middle line:84%
have a triumphant slide
that I want to show you.

01:13:25.630 --> 01:13:29.390 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe we can
unsquelch you and come back.

01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:33.640 align:middle line:84%
So here's the idea, just so
it's clearer for everybody.

01:13:33.640 --> 01:13:35.350 align:middle line:84%
For the easy case,
the one that I

01:13:35.350 --> 01:13:36.880 align:middle line:84%
want to refocus
your attention on,

01:13:36.880 --> 01:13:39.850 align:middle line:84%
"I will find the book in the
garage," what we want to do

01:13:39.850 --> 01:13:42.100 align:middle line:84%
is construct a
structure for this

01:13:42.100 --> 01:13:44.710 align:middle line:84%
that's sensitive to all
of the tests for structure

01:13:44.710 --> 01:13:46.210 align:middle line:84%
that we've been
developing, and it's

01:13:46.210 --> 01:13:50.140 align:middle line:84%
going to involve putting things
together via pairwise merge

01:13:50.140 --> 01:13:54.100 align:middle line:84%
and creating labels
for the things

01:13:54.100 --> 01:13:58.420 align:middle line:84%
that we create via pairwise
merge that are typically

01:13:58.420 --> 01:14:01.510 align:middle line:84%
labels that come from one of the
two things that we've merged.

01:14:01.510 --> 01:14:04.158 align:middle line:84%
So when we merged "the
book" or "the garage,"

01:14:04.158 --> 01:14:05.950 align:middle line:84%
we're going to create
something we're going

01:14:05.950 --> 01:14:08.050 align:middle line:90%
to give the label "noun" to.

01:14:08.050 --> 01:14:11.587 align:middle line:84%
Or when we merge "in"
together with "the garage,"

01:14:11.587 --> 01:14:13.420 align:middle line:84%
we're going to create
what I've been calling

01:14:13.420 --> 01:14:15.462 align:middle line:84%
a prepositional phrase,
something that we'll give

01:14:15.462 --> 01:14:18.370 align:middle line:84%
the label "p" to, saying
this has the properties

01:14:18.370 --> 01:14:22.120 align:middle line:84%
that it has-- it contains
a preposition "in."

01:14:22.120 --> 01:14:25.690 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, we'll combine
"find" with "the book"

01:14:25.690 --> 01:14:29.110 align:middle line:84%
and we'll combine "find the
book" with "in the garage,"

01:14:29.110 --> 01:14:32.110 align:middle line:84%
and we'll end up with
a structure like that.

01:14:32.110 --> 01:14:34.510 align:middle line:84%
Is anybody shocked
by this slide?

01:14:34.510 --> 01:14:36.670 align:middle line:90%
You are, yes.

01:14:36.670 --> 01:14:37.900 align:middle line:90%
What did you want to do?

01:14:37.900 --> 01:14:40.420 align:middle line:90%


01:14:40.420 --> 01:14:42.950 align:middle line:90%
You're gesturing.

01:14:42.950 --> 01:14:45.780 align:middle line:84%
You want to combine
things in a different way?

01:14:45.780 --> 01:14:50.120 align:middle line:90%
So actually, you may be right.

01:14:50.120 --> 01:14:52.370 align:middle line:90%
No, here's a better way to say--

01:14:52.370 --> 01:14:54.380 align:middle line:90%
we may both be right.

01:14:54.380 --> 01:14:58.880 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example where
this way of merging things

01:14:58.880 --> 01:15:00.750 align:middle line:90%
creates a well-formed structure.

01:15:00.750 --> 01:15:04.820 align:middle line:84%
Notice that if I say, "I will
find the book in the garage,"

01:15:04.820 --> 01:15:05.600 align:middle line:90%
it's OK.

01:15:05.600 --> 01:15:07.970 align:middle line:84%
"I said I would find
the book in the garage.

01:15:07.970 --> 01:15:10.280 align:middle line:84%
And in the garage, I
will find the book."

01:15:10.280 --> 01:15:13.130 align:middle line:90%


01:15:13.130 --> 01:15:15.257 align:middle line:84%
That's a unit that
topicalization

01:15:15.257 --> 01:15:16.340 align:middle line:90%
gets to make reference to.

01:15:16.340 --> 01:15:17.690 align:middle line:90%
That's a property of this tree.

01:15:17.690 --> 01:15:21.700 align:middle line:84%
So our tests get to
tease this out now.

01:15:21.700 --> 01:15:23.648 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm regretting us
being almost out of time

01:15:23.648 --> 01:15:25.190 align:middle line:84%
because there are
many things to say.

01:15:25.190 --> 01:15:28.070 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to
see next is that when

01:15:28.070 --> 01:15:31.200 align:middle line:84%
we construct syntactic
trees for strings of words,

01:15:31.200 --> 01:15:33.230 align:middle line:84%
it's often the case
that we get ambiguities

01:15:33.230 --> 01:15:36.320 align:middle line:84%
like the "unlockable" ambiguity,
places where there is more

01:15:36.320 --> 01:15:37.803 align:middle line:90%
than one way to combine things.

01:15:37.803 --> 01:15:40.220 align:middle line:84%
And I think you may be looking
at this slide and thinking,

01:15:40.220 --> 01:15:42.290 align:middle line:84%
oh, but there's another
way to combine these words,

01:15:42.290 --> 01:15:43.457 align:middle line:90%
and you're absolutely right.

01:15:43.457 --> 01:15:44.120 align:middle line:90%
There is.

01:15:44.120 --> 01:15:45.770 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll do
is develop tests

01:15:45.770 --> 01:15:47.860 align:middle line:84%
that allow us to see
which way we've combined

01:15:47.860 --> 01:15:48.860 align:middle line:90%
words in different ways.

01:15:48.860 --> 01:15:50.690 align:middle line:84%
And we'll find cases
like "unlockable"

01:15:50.690 --> 01:15:53.720 align:middle line:84%
where, depending on in what
order you combine things,

01:15:53.720 --> 01:15:55.830 align:middle line:84%
you get different
meanings, and our tests

01:15:55.830 --> 01:15:57.440 align:middle line:90%
will combine with that.

01:15:57.440 --> 01:15:58.500 align:middle line:90%
Yep?

01:15:58.500 --> 01:15:59.000 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:15:59.000 --> 01:15:59.810 align:middle line:90%
Thanks, everybody.

01:15:59.810 --> 01:16:03.010 align:middle line:84%
We will do this
again on Tuesday.

01:16:03.010 --> 01:16:07.000 align:middle line:90%